-- allium: 3
-- mcp-task-tools.allium
--
-- Scope: MCP tools for task management (create, get, update, list,
--        dispatch, resume, query-usage,
--        set-verify-command) and the CLI plan-attachment surface; the
--        standard every tool description and agent-facing skill is held to;
--        context via MCP — the read-only resources the dispatch MCP server
--        offers (skills and their references/ files, validated learnings,
--        the caller's own task) through
--        resources/list, resources/read and their tool twin read_context.
-- Excludes: Task lifecycle (see tasks.allium); dispatch/retry (see dispatch.allium);
--           MCP epic tools (see epics.allium); learnings tools (see learnings.allium);
--           work-package tools (see work-packages.allium);
--           agent-to-agent
--           messaging, which is no longer a dispatch MCP tool — see
--           HookPeerMessageSent in agent-health.allium.

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./schedules.allium" as schedules

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Most entries one resources/list page returns; further entries are
    -- reached through nextCursor (ListContextResourcesViaMcp).
    context_list_page_size: Integer = 50

    -- How long dispatch_task waits on its dispatch request before returning
    -- the request id and its pending state (DispatchTaskViaMcp). Shorter than
    -- the request's own deadline (store-split.allium:
    -- config.dispatch_request_timeout), so a slow provisioning returns a
    -- pending request rather than holding the MCP call open to the end.
    dispatch_task_wait_cap: Duration = 30.seconds
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Tool descriptions ==
--
-- A tool's `description` and its arguments' descriptions are an agent-facing
-- surface, held to the same standard as the specs and docs: they state
-- behaviour, and they name no code. A description may name a tool, an argument
-- or a setting the caller passes — that is public interface — but never the
-- function, type, macro or file behind it. Two reasons the bar is *higher*
-- here than in docs/: check-doc-symbols.sh scans src/ for doc comments only
-- (`///` and `//!`), so a description — a string literal — is invisible to it
-- and nothing re-checks a citation that goes stale; and a description reaches
-- an agent mid-task, where it is acted on rather than read.
--
-- Individual rules below add their own description guarantees where a wrong or
-- missing sentence has a named consequence. Those are asserted in tests, and
-- derived from the generated schema wherever the fact has a machine-readable
-- source, so the description cannot drift from the tool it describes.
--
-- A description says what the tool does and what it returns, and stops.
-- Everything else an agent needs has a better home, and a copy in the
-- description is a second wording nothing keeps in step:
--
--   - What to do NEXT goes in the tool's RESPONSE, where it arrives just in
--     time and only on the path that needs it. wrap_up's response names
--     exit_session as the final call; neither tool's description repeats it
--     (see WrapUpViaMcp: TheNextCallIsNamedJustInTime).
--   - Why a call is REFUSED goes in the refusal's error message, which is
--     the only text a refused caller is certain to read
--     (ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp).
--   - A constraint the schema can express is expressed by the schema, not
--     prose: an enum for a closed value set, a conditional requirement for
--     one argument depending on another (update_tasks' url_type).
--   - An argument that means the same thing on every tool that takes it —
--     base_branch, wrap_up_mode, model, auto_run_plan, phoenix, tag, sort_order —
--     carries one short wording of that meaning, the same on each tool. A
--     tool adds text only where its meaning differs (an omitted argument
--     defaulting on create but meaning "no change" on update).
--   - A story spanning several tools is told once, by the tool where the
--     caller acts on it: resuming a crashed task is dispatch_task's
--     (DispatchTaskViaMcp), not update_tasks' status argument's.
--   - Authoring rules for a tool's content live in the skill that teaches
--     them; the description points at the skill (record_learning and the
--     /learnings skill — learnings.allium: RecordLearningViaMcp).
--   - Human-UI trivia (which tags the TUI offers), implementation detail
--     (how a self-reference is guarded) and worked examples (list_keybindings
--     once showed one) do not belong. A caller acts on neither of the first
--     two, and the schema plus a real response already show the third.
--
-- == Skill copy ==
--
-- The agent-facing skills (the /wrap-up, /retro, /allium-loop, /fix and /learnings
-- skills and their siblings) follow the same just-in-time principle. A skill's
-- SKILL.md carries the flow every invocation walks. Detail only one branch
-- needs lives in a references/ file beside it, and the branch names that file
-- at the point it is taken, so the file is loaded only on that path. The
-- /wrap-up skill's pr path and its exit_session error handling are such
-- branches (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpPr, ExitSession); /retro, /allium-loop
-- and /learnings keep their core flow in SKILL.md and move their long tails
-- the same way.
--
-- The same SKILL.md files, and the references/ files beside them, are also
-- served as MCP resources (see "MCP context resources" below), from the copy
-- built into dispatch out of plugin/skills — never from the installed copy
-- under ~/.claude/plugins/. So a skill's copy reaches an agent by two routes,
-- both carry the text in plugin/skills, and a branch that names a references/
-- file can be followed over MCP too.
--
-- The /fix skill creates and dispatches a task in the repo that owns a defect.
-- It checks for a duplicate and asks the user to confirm before create_task,
-- because dispatch launches an agent in auto mode and a dispatched agent may
-- invoke /fix itself; the confirmation is the only bound on that chain.

-- == Tool definitions follow the authoring standard ==
--
-- Anthropic's guidance on tool definitions (platform.claude.com "Define
-- tools"; anthropic.com/engineering "Writing tools for agents") is the
-- reference for every tool. docs/mcp-tool-design.md is how this repo applies it
-- and holds the audit of the current tool list. The parts a test can hold:
--   - a description of at least three sentences (the detail floor, asserted by
--     every_tool_description_meets_the_detail_floor);
--   - a description on every argument, so no caller guesses what one means;
--   - behaviour annotations on every tool, so a client can tell a read from a
--     write from a destructive call without parsing prose.

rule ToolsAdvertiseAnnotations {
    when: McpToolsList()

    -- Every tool in the list carries `annotations` with a `title` and the four
    -- MCP behaviour hints. readOnlyHint is true exactly for a tool that changes
    -- nothing (get_task, list_tasks, list_epics, query_learnings,
    -- query_usage, read_context, list_keybindings, list_work_packages).
    -- managed_feed_config is NOT in that list: its no-argument call reads but
    -- its other calls write, so it is declared idempotent (see
    -- epics.allium: ManagedFeedConfigViaMcp, @guidance).
    -- destructiveHint is true only where a call removes or closes something
    -- that cannot be restored by another call (delete_learning, exit_session,
    -- update_work_package, whose delete form removes the package);
    -- it is false on a read-only tool. idempotentHint is true where repeating
    -- the identical call leaves the same state. openWorldHint is false on every
    -- tool: dispatch reaches no system outside itself through them.
    ensures: ToolsAnnotated()

    @guidance
        -- The access class is declared in the mcp_tools! entry beside the tool's
        -- name (read_only, write, idempotent or destructive), so a new tool
        -- cannot be registered without choosing one.
        -- Asserted by tool_definitions_carry_annotations and
        -- every_argument_carries_a_description in src/mcp/handlers/tests/mod.rs.
}

-- == The agent port answers loopback callers only ==
--
-- A web page can rebind a hostname it controls to 127.0.0.1 and then reach the
-- agent port same-origin, with no CORS preflight to stop it. Caller identity is
-- a client-supplied header, so the port itself must refuse such a request.
-- This covers /mcp, the hook path and the pane-view path.

rule AgentPortRejectsNonLoopbackOrigins {
    when: AgentPortRequest(host?, origin?)

    -- host and origin are the request's Host and Origin headers; "loopback"
    -- means the authority parses strictly (no userinfo, path or non-numeric
    -- port; scheme http or https for an origin) and the hostname is 127.0.0.1,
    -- localhost or [::1], case-insensitively and with no trailing dot. Any port
    -- present must be the port the board is bound to. A Host may omit the port
    -- (the hook client sends Host "127.0.0.1"); an Origin may not, since a
    -- browser always sends it and a missing one means the default port. "null"
    -- is not loopback. A request with no Host header is allowed: a browser
    -- always sends one.
    ensures:
        if (host != null and not loopback(host)) or (origin != null and not loopback(origin)):
            RequestRejected(status: 403)

    @guidance
        -- The check runs before caller identity is read, so a rejected request
        -- never reaches a handler. A per-board bearer token is a possible
        -- follow-up and is not part of this rule.
}

-- == The hook path takes JSON only ==
--
-- A cross-origin text/plain POST needs no preflight, so a body on the hook
-- path that is not declared application/json (charset parameters allowed) is
-- refused with 415 and never applied.

-- == MCP tool failures share one shape ==
--
-- Every failure after a tools/call is accepted, including an unresolvable
-- agent name in the arguments, comes back as a result with isError: true and
-- the message as text. A tool can never return a JSON-RPC error code. Only an
-- unreadable request (bad identity) is a JSON-RPC error. This includes a
-- task_id that names no task ("Task N not found"). A missing or null
-- `arguments` means an empty object.

-- == MCP Task Tools ==

-- UpdateTaskViaMcp is the per-task rule behind the one update tool,
-- update_tasks (UpdateTasksViaMcp below). There is no separate update_task
-- tool: a call with one id is the single-task update.
rule UpdateTaskViaMcp {
    when: McpUpdateTask(task, status?, sub_status?, plan?, title?, description?,
                        repo_path?, sort_order?, url?, url_type?, tag?, epic_id?,
                        base_branch?, wrap_up_mode?, model?, auto_run_plan?, phoenix?,
                        schedule?)

    requires: status != null or sub_status != null or plan != null or title != null
              or description != null or repo_path != null
              or sort_order != null or epic_id != null
              or url != null or tag != null
              or base_branch != null
              or wrap_up_mode != omitted
              or model != omitted
              or auto_run_plan != null
              or phoenix != null
              or schedule != null

    -- Typed-url validation contract. The url string and its type travel
    -- together; the handler rejects inconsistent combinations before any
    -- mutation occurs. The advertised schema states the same contract — an
    -- enum for url_type, and url_type required whenever url is non-empty —
    -- so the argument descriptions carry no prose restating it.
    --   • A non-empty url REQUIRES url_type (an unaccompanied url is rejected).
    --   • An unknown url_type (not one of UrlType's literals) is rejected —
    --     whether or not url is present. url_type is parsed into its enum at
    --     the JSON-RPC boundary, like status, tag and sub_status, so a bad
    --     literal fails argument decoding before this rule is reached and is
    --     never silently ignored on a url-less call.
    requires: url_type = null or url_type_is_valid(url_type)
    requires:
        if url != null and url != "":
            url_type != null

    -- Deletion is never reachable over MCP, on any path: there is no MCP
    -- delete tool. Permanent delete stays a human TUI action (tasks.allium:
    -- DeleteTask), because it cannot be undone and it retires a feed item.
    --
    -- Done is now reachable via MCP, but never through THIS rule — it is
    -- carved out entirely into MarkTaskDoneViaMcp below, a dedicated
    -- close-only call (task_id and status="done", nothing else) with its
    -- own caller guard. `status != done` here means exactly that: this
    -- generic multi-field rule refuses status="done" unconditionally,
    -- regardless of what else the call sets, so a call combining
    -- status="done" with any other field never partially matches here —
    -- it is rejected by MarkTaskDoneViaMcp's own no-other-fields guard
    -- instead, with one consistent error message.
    --
    -- These two guards constrain the TARGET status only — task.status is
    -- never read to validate the move. This rule is therefore exempt from
    -- core/Task's status transition graph (see core.allium:
    -- Task.transitions status): any status other than done is accepted
    -- from any current status, with no edge check. Deliberate: agents
    -- legitimately re-open and re-queue their own work
    -- (`review -> backlog`, `done -> backlog`), which the graph does not
    -- declare.
    requires: status != done

    -- sub_status must be valid for the task's current (or new) status
    --
    -- ENFORCED BY THE STORE (store-split.allium M2, task #53255): the
    -- patch_task reducer checks the sub_status against the status the row
    -- will hold after the patch, so the verdict is the same for every client
    -- and is read from the true row, not a subscription view. The service
    -- passes the sub_status through. The refusal reads "sub_status 'x' is not
    -- valid for status 'y'" and reaches the caller as an invalid-params error.
    -- The same table now binds every store writer that moves a sub_status,
    -- not only this patch (store-split.allium: SubStatusMovesMustBeLegal).
    -- `sub_status_valid_for(sub_status, status)` is a black box naming the
    -- store's legality table, the pairs listed in core.allium under "Valid
    -- SubStatus values per parent status".
    requires:
        if sub_status != null:
            let effective_status = if status != null: status else: task.status
            sub_status_valid_for(sub_status, effective_status)

    -- The MCP tool's advertised sub_status enum deliberately omits pr_closed
    -- and pr_unreachable: both are derived from GitHub PR polling
    -- (pr-workflow.allium: PollPrStatus, PrPollGaveUp) — neither is a value an
    -- agent should choose to set. This is advertisement-only — the rule above
    -- still accepts either if a caller sends it anyway, same as any other
    -- SubStatus valid for the effective status.

    let prior_status = task.status

    ensures:
        if status != null: task.status = status
    ensures:
        if sub_status != null: task.sub_status = sub_status
        else if status != null: task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(status)
    ensures:
        if plan != null: task.plan_path = plan
    ensures:
        if title != null: task.title = title
    ensures:
        if description != null: task.description = description
    ensures:
        if repo_path != null: task.repo_path = expand_tilde(repo_path)
    ensures:
        if sort_order != null: task.sort_order = sort_order
    ensures:
        -- Leaving running voids any deferred Stop, exactly as the manual
        -- moves in tasks.allium do. Without this an agent that moves its own
        -- task out of running and later back in would carry a Stop from the
        -- earlier turn, which the next drain would apply against the new one.
        -- See core/Task's PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning invariant. Since
        -- store-split M7 the module's patch_task applies this clear in the
        -- same write (store-split.allium, X2); the board adds nothing.
        if status != null and status != running and prior_status = running:
            current_agent(task).stop_pending = false
    ensures:
        if url != null:
            if url = "":
                task.url = null      -- empty string clears the url (and its type)
            else:
                task.url = core/TaskUrl{url: url, url_type: url_type}
    ensures:
        if tag != null: task.tag = tag
    ensures:
        if epic_id != null: task.epic = route_target(core/Epic.find(epic_id), task.repo_path)
    ensures:
        if base_branch != null: task.base_branch = base_branch
    ensures:
        -- wrap_up_mode supports three states: omitted (no change), one of
        -- WrapUpMode's literals (set), or explicit null (clear to NULL).
        if wrap_up_mode != omitted: task.wrap_up_mode = wrap_up_mode
    ensures:
        -- model has the same three states as wrap_up_mode: omitted (no
        -- change), one of TaskModel's literals (set), or explicit null (clear,
        -- so the task launches on sonnet again — tasks.allium: SetModel).
        if model != omitted: task.model = model
    ensures:
        if auto_run_plan != null: task.auto_run_plan = auto_run_plan
        -- phoenix is a plain nullable boolean: omitted leaves it alone, true
        -- and false set it. What it then does is PhoenixRespawn
        -- (tasks.allium) — this rule itself cannot set status to done, so a
        -- call through THIS rule can only arm or disarm the recurrence, never
        -- trigger it. MarkTaskDoneViaMcp below is a different matter: it CAN
        -- reach done (on a task other than the calling agent's own), and
        -- because the module's close (store-split.allium: RequestCloseTask)
        -- runs the respawn on entering Done as exit_session's close does, a
        -- phoenix-armed task closed that way respawns — see that rule's own
        -- guidance.
        if phoenix != null: task.phoenix = phoenix
    ensures:
        -- schedule is a five-field UTC cron string (schedules.allium). The
        -- empty string clears it; omitted leaves it alone. Applied after
        -- phoenix in the same write, so one call can arm both and
        -- SetSchedule's backlog-or-phoenix check sees the new flag.
        if schedule != null:
            if schedule = "": schedules/ScheduleCleared(task: task)
            else: schedules/ScheduleSet(task: task, cron: schedule)
    ensures: task.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- MCP update_tasks accepts status, sub_status, plan_path, title,
        -- description, repo_path, sort_order, url, url_type, tag, epic_id,
        -- base_branch, wrap_up_mode, model, auto_run_plan, phoenix, and schedule. It does NOT
        -- accept worktree or tmux_window — those are owned by the dispatch flow
        -- and never set via MCP.
        --
        -- == The tool description enumerates every field it accepts ==
        -- update_tasks' description opens with that list, in full, and it is
        -- the first thing a caller reads. The list drifted once — it named
        -- eight of the fifteen fields and still called the typed url pair "PR
        -- fields" — and an unadvertised field is one a caller reaches for a
        -- second tool to do, or does not do at all. The list is asserted
        -- against the generated schema rather than reviewed by eye, so a field
        -- added to the argument set fails until the description learns it.
        -- wrap_up_mode can be set to "rebase", "pr", or "done" to pre-set
        -- the wrap-up action for the task. Pass null explicitly to clear it
        -- (omitting the field is a no-op).
        -- auto_run_plan (boolean, not nullable at the Task level) can be
        -- set via create_task or update_tasks. Omitting it is a no-op on
        -- update; create_task defaults it to false when absent.
        -- phoenix behaves the same way, and is the one field here an agent is
        -- expected to set on ITSELF: a dispatched agent that works out the task
        -- it is running is a recurring one calls update_tasks(task_ids: [id], phoenix: true) and
        -- the recurrence survives its own session. Arming it costs nothing if
        -- wrong — the flag does nothing until the task reaches done, and the
        -- human can clear it from the editor.
        -- Note: worktree and tmux_window ARE mutable through the internal
        -- Rust TaskService::update_task API (UpdateTaskParams defines
        -- them; see src/service/tasks/params.rs). The runtime uses that
        -- path to clear the pointers once a teardown has released them, and
        -- for the post-provision patch on both dispatch paths (dispatch_task
        -- and the epic chain).
        -- They are intentionally unreachable from MCP because the MCP
        -- handler does not parse those fields: the UpdateTaskArgs struct
        -- that defines the accepted argument set
        -- (src/mcp/handlers/tasks/mod.rs) has no worktree or
        -- tmux_window field, so handle_update_task
        -- (src/mcp/handlers/tasks/crud.rs) can never carry them into
        -- UpdateTaskParams.
        -- The typed url is carried by two params: `url` (the string) and
        -- `url_type` (one of pr | security_alert | issue | other). A non-empty
        -- `url` REQUIRES `url_type`; an unknown `url_type` is rejected
        -- unconditionally, at argument decoding. Both facts are stated by the
        -- schema (see the typed-url contract above), not by prose. An
        -- empty-string `url` clears task.url and its type (maps to
        -- FieldUpdate::Clear); any non-empty `url` sets task.url to a
        -- core/TaskUrl{url, url_type} value.
        -- This rule writes nothing on a status change out of done. It used to
        -- clear a completion-recency rank from task.sort_order; that rank is
        -- now task.completed_at, which is deliberately kept across a move out
        -- of done (tasks.allium, ConfirmDone's guidance), and sort_order is
        -- left to mean manual ordering alone. So an explicit sort_order in the
        -- same call is no longer overridden by the status param: the two write
        -- different fields. The entering-done stamp is not reachable from this
        -- rule at all — `requires: status != done` rules it out — and is
        -- applied on the other routes into done: tasks/ConfirmDone (the TUI
        -- path, through the module's patch_task, which stamps completed_at on
        -- entering Done) and MarkTaskDoneViaMcp below (the MCP path, through
        -- the module's close_task; store-split.allium: RequestCloseTask).
        -- Since store-split M7 the board stamps nothing itself (X2): a board
        -- that needs the stamp re-reads the task.
        -- The `status` argument's description lists the statuses and nothing
        -- more. It used to carry the resume-a-crashed-task story and an aside
        -- that no MCP path deletes a task; the first is dispatch_task's
        -- (DispatchTaskViaMcp owns it, once), and the second describes a tool
        -- that does not exist, which no caller acts on.
        -- When status or epic_id changes, the parent epic's status is
        -- recalculated (if a parent epic exists). Re-assigning a task
        -- from one epic to another triggers recalculation on both the
        -- old and new epic (the store's set_task_epic does this itself).
        -- When epic_id refers to a grouped root (group_by_repo = true),
        -- route_target redirects the task to the per-repo sub-epic for
        -- task.repo_path, creating the sub-epic if needed. When epic_id
        -- refers to a specific sub-epic, the assignment is direct.
        -- origin is never user-supplied: it is a system field set only
        -- by the store's repo-grouping reducers. Callers
        -- cannot set or observe origin via MCP.
        -- When repo_path is changed alongside or independently, and the
        -- task sits inside a grouped (non-feed) subtree, the store's
        -- patch_task moves it to the correct sub-epic for the new repo_path
        -- and deletes the sub-epic it left if that is now empty
        -- (epics.allium's routing note above RegroupEpic).
        --
        -- ONE CALL. The service sends an explicit epic_id and the patch
        -- (repo_path, sub_status, status, the rest) to the store as one
        -- update_task call (store-split.allium: UpdateTaskIsOneTransaction),
        -- one transaction: a patch the store refuses leaves the epic, the
        -- repo and every other field as they were. A call that changes both
        -- epic_id (to a grouped root) and repo_path lands where routing by the
        -- NEW repo_path puts it. The epic_id never rides in patch_task, which
        -- refuses one (store-split.allium: PatchTaskNeverMovesAnEpic).
}

rule MarkTaskDoneViaMcp {
    when: McpUpdateTask(task, caller_identity, status?, sub_status?, plan?, title?,
                        description?, repo_path?, sort_order?, url?, url_type?, tag?,
                        epic_id?, base_branch?, wrap_up_mode?, model?, auto_run_plan?,
                        phoenix?, schedule?)

    requires: status = done

    -- A dedicated close-only call: no other field may travel with
    -- status="done" in the same update_tasks call — not even one that would
    -- be a harmless no-op elsewhere. url_type is a case in point: everywhere
    -- else this tool accepts it, url_type has no effect without a non-empty
    -- url — it "folds into url" (see UpdateTaskViaMcp's guidance) rather than
    -- owning a Task field of its own — but sending it alongside status="done"
    -- is still rejected here, same as any other field. A close-only call
    -- silently discarding an argument the caller bothered to send is a worse
    -- failure mode than a slightly stricter guard; a caller wanting both a
    -- field change and completion makes two calls — one update_tasks call for
    -- the field, a separate status-only call to close.
    requires:
        sub_status = null and plan = null and title = null and description = null
        and repo_path = null and sort_order = null and url = null and url_type = null
        and tag = null and epic_id = null and base_branch = null
        and wrap_up_mode = omitted and model = omitted and auto_run_plan = null
        and phoenix = null
        and schedule = null

    -- A dispatched agent may not close its OWN task through this call — that
    -- route stays wrap_up + exit_session, with its token, its ordering
    -- guarantees and its epic chain all in one place (ExitSession,
    -- pr-workflow.allium). A session caller (a human's own Claude Code
    -- session, or a script/orchestrator with no dispatched identity of its
    -- own) may close ANY task this way, including one with a live tmux
    -- window. A dispatched agent may likewise close any task OTHER than the
    -- one it is running as — e.g. a triage/orchestrating agent closing
    -- sibling cards — but never itself.
    requires:
        not (caller_identity.kind = task and caller_identity.task_id = task.id)

    -- No requirement on task.status: reachable from any current status
    -- (backlog, running, review, even done), the same
    -- no-edge-check philosophy UpdateTaskViaMcp already applies to its own
    -- target statuses. A task with no live tmux_window (never dispatched, or
    -- already detached) closes with nothing to tear down.

    -- Since store-split M7 the tool ASKS and the module closes: one
    -- close_task call naming this board's host (store-split.allium:
    -- RequestCloseTask, X1). In one transaction the module writes Done (a
    -- done -> done re-close keeps the task's completed_at; every other prior
    -- status gets the fresh stamp), clears stop_pending when the task was
    -- running and clears its tmux_window, rolls up the epic chain, respawns a
    -- phoenix task entering Done, fails any open session request on the task
    -- ("task closed"), and writes a close row for the host's teardown when
    -- the task had a window or carries a pr-typed url. The close either
    -- landed whole or not at all: a task whose close did not land keeps its
    -- prior status, sub_status, completed_at and any live window. (No
    -- SessionClosed is emitted on this path on either outcome — see the note
    -- after the ensures.)
    ensures: CloseTaskRequested(task: task, host: core/local_host())

    -- No SessionClosed, therefore no epic chain. This is where this path
    -- deliberately diverges from ExitSession: the terminal fields and a tmux
    -- teardown are common to both (applied by the module and torn down by the
    -- host on both routes, for exit_session since store-split M5 and for this
    -- path since M7), the chain is not.
    --
    -- Why. SessionClosed is the signal AutoDispatchNextSubtask (epics.allium)
    -- keys on, and the chain's whole value is that the successor starts from a
    -- base_branch already carrying the closed task's work. Only exit_session
    -- can promise that: it is reachable exclusively through wrap_up, which
    -- rebased onto base_branch, opened a PR, or was told explicitly that the
    -- task needed neither. This call has no wrap_up ahead of it — no rebase,
    -- no PR, no exit token, and (by its own requires) no relationship at all
    -- between the caller and the work being closed. A chain fired from here
    -- launches an agent against whatever base_branch happened to hold, on the
    -- say-so of a caller who was never in that task's session.
    --
    -- The epic is not stuck, and this is not a stall: it is the same untouched
    -- epic the chain's other normal stopping conditions produce, with the same
    -- recovery — the human dispatches the next subtask from the TUI. So it
    -- raises no AutoDispatchFailed and shows no chain-stalled marker; nothing
    -- failed.

    @guidance
        -- The MCP `update_tasks` tool's dedicated done-only path. Handled by
        -- handle_update_task (src/mcp/handlers/tasks/crud.rs), which checks
        -- `status = "done"` FIRST — before the url/url_type pairing
        -- validation, before any field mapping —
        -- and branches to a separate function operating on the raw parsed
        -- arguments, not on the built UpdateTaskParams. That ordering is load
        -- bearing: checking the field-exclusivity guard against
        -- UpdateTaskParams instead (which has no slot for a url_type sent
        -- without a url) would let such a call slip through unrejected, and
        -- checking it after the url/url_type pairing validation would answer
        -- a `status="done", url=<x>` call with "url_type is required" instead
        -- of the close-only error this rule promises.
        --
        -- From there, this path calls the close_task reducer
        -- (store-split.allium: RequestCloseTask) and nothing else. Until
        -- store-split M7 it closed in the board instead, through
        -- allow-phantom-symbol: removed in M7, history
        -- close_session and kill_session_window via a `perform_close` helper
        -- that exit_session shared before M5; between M5 and M7 the two
        -- closes applied the same terminal fields by two implementations.
        -- M7 removed that board-side close (close_session, perform_close and
        -- its close_persisted gate): both closes are now module
        -- transactions, and both teardowns are the host's, from a session
        -- request row (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown). Only the
        -- request validation (the two requires above), the response text,
        -- the missing token and the missing epic chain are specific to this
        -- path.
        --
        -- The chain is not part of this path at all. It is not suppressed by
        -- a flag: since M5 it runs inside the module's exit_session
        -- transaction (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask), which this
        -- path never reaches. See the no-SessionClosed note above for why
        -- this path must not chain.
        --
        -- No exit token is issued, checked, or consumed anywhere on this
        -- path — unlike exit_session, there is no wrap_up call first. That is
        -- the whole point: it is a distinct, deliberately simpler route to
        -- Done for a caller who was never going to call wrap_up on this task
        -- in the first place (a session closing a batch of finished cards, or
        -- an orchestrating agent closing a sibling task).
        --
        -- Validation order (both are ServiceError::Validation, -32602),
        -- entirely ahead of UpdateTaskViaMcp's own url-pairing
        -- checks, which never run for a status="done" call:
        --   1. Any field other than status set alongside status="done"
        --      (url_type included, even though it is inert everywhere else
        --      this tool accepts it without an accompanying url — see the
        --      requires clause above) →
        --      "status=\"done\" must be the only field set in this call
        --      (also set: <names>). Update other fields separately, then
        --      close with a status-only call."
        --   2. caller_identity is Task(t) and t = the target task_id →
        --      "Cannot mark your own task done via update_tasks — call
        --      wrap_up then exit_session instead."
        --   3. Unknown task_id → the same NotFound(-32001) every other MCP
        --      task lookup uses ("Task <id> not found").
        --      The check is the service's own existence read, made before
        --      any write, on every store. On this path the close_task reducer
        --      refuses a missing task too, but in its own words, which would
        --      reach the caller only as the could-not-move-to-done reply
        --      below; the read is what gives the NotFound answer. (On the
        --      other update_tasks paths the read is what keeps a missing task
        --      from looking like success at all: the shared store's
        --      patch_task reducer is a deliberate silent no-op on a missing id,
        --      since hooks race with deletes. The same holds for the TUI's
        --      sub_status write, which goes through update_tasks.) A task
        --      deleted between the read and the close gets the
        --      could-not-move reply.
        -- A caller could trip both 1 and 2 at once; 1 is checked first, so
        -- that error names the field-combination problem regardless of who
        -- is calling.
        --
        -- close_task has no precondition on prior status or on tmux_window
        -- being present — it is called unconditionally once validation
        -- passes, so a backlog task with no window closes exactly as cleanly
        -- as a running task with a live one. A task that enters Done through
        -- it respawns if it is a phoenix task — the same PhoenixRespawn
        -- (tasks.allium) any other route into Done triggers, applied in the
        -- close's own transaction. An open session request on the task (a
        -- wrap_up rebase still running, say) does not block the close: the
        -- module fails it "task closed" in the same transaction.
        --
        -- When the close does NOT land (the call is refused or the store
        -- fails it): this call reports the task could not be moved to done
        -- and invites a retry (worded differently from ExitSessionViaMcp's
        -- no-answer text, which names a live tmux session and a still-valid
        -- token — this path may have no session at all to speak of); nothing
        -- was written, no close row exists, and any live window is left
        -- alive — the task is exactly as it was before the call. A call that
        -- got NO ANSWER from the store gets the same reply, although its
        -- close may have landed: unlike ExitSessionViaMcp, this path does not
        -- settle a no-answer by re-reading (an open question in
        -- store-split.allium). A retry then re-closes a task already Done,
        -- which restamps and respawns nothing. When it DOES
        -- land: the host serving the close row kills the window (if any) and
        -- closes a pr-typed url's PR, best effort, after the call has
        -- returned; the tool waits for neither. Where the window is on a
        -- machine this caller does not own, no row is written and the window
        -- is left to its own machine (store-split.allium, X1). Boards learn of
        -- the close through their subscriptions; this board also sends its
        -- own task-changed notification (and epic-changed, for a task in an
        -- epic) after the call, on either outcome. The response text is
        -- unchanged by M7. No epic auto-dispatch chain runs on either
        -- outcome, so the response text never names a chained subtask —
        -- unlike exit_session's, which does.
        --
        -- MCP_UPDATABLE (src/models/tasks.rs) now includes Done: the
        -- schema's advertised status enum is backlog | running | review |
        -- done, which is every TaskStatus there is. There is no delete
        -- counterpart to MarkTaskDoneViaMcp: removing a task is a human TUI
        -- action (tasks.allium: DeleteTask).
}

rule PrFinalisationReflectionNudge {
    when: McpUpdateTask(task, status?, sub_status?, plan?, title?, description?,
                        repo_path?, sort_order?, url?, url_type?, tag?, epic_id?,
                        base_branch?, wrap_up_mode?, auto_run_plan?)

    -- "PR finalisation": this one call both moves the task to review and
    -- attaches a pr-typed url, on a task that carried no url at all before the
    -- call. Preconditions read pre-call state, so task.url = null is the
    -- first-time-a-PR-is-linked condition, not a post-update assertion.
    requires: status = review
    requires: url != null and url != "" and url_type = pr
    requires: task.url = null

    requires: core/config.learning_reflection_enabled = true

    ensures: ReflectionNudgeAppended(task: task)

    @guidance
        -- The sole consumer of core/config.learning_reflection_enabled. The
        -- nudge is a sentence appended to the update_tasks success response
        -- text, asking the agent whether it discovered anything non-obvious
        -- about the repo or task and, if so, to call record_learning with a
        -- brief summary. It never gates or alters the update: UpdateTaskViaMcp
        -- applies its mutations regardless, and when the flag is false the
        -- response text is simply shorter.
        --
        -- Layering: the finalisation shape is computed in the service layer as
        -- was_pr_finalisation (src/service/tasks/crud.rs) — status = review AND
        -- a pr-typed url AND the pre-write task had url = None — and returned on
        -- UpdateTaskResult. handle_update_task (src/mcp/handlers/tasks/crud.rs)
        -- consults it and only then calls reflection_nudge
        -- (src/mcp/handlers/tasks/mod.rs), which reads the setting and returns
        -- the empty string when it is false.
        --
        -- IMPORTANT — this nudge does NOT fire on the normal wrap-up path.
        -- ExitSessionViaMcp (below) performs an equivalent mutation for
        -- action = pr (status -> review plus a pr-typed url, set
        -- unconditionally regardless of any prior url), but that close is the
        -- module's (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSession, run inside
        -- store-split.allium's RequestExitSession since M5; before M5 the
        -- board's close_session), which computes no was_pr_finalisation, and
        -- handle_exit_session (src/mcp/handlers/tasks/wrap_up.rs) never calls
        -- reflection_nudge. So the nudge only appears when a caller invokes the
        -- update_tasks MCP tool directly to link a PR, bypassing
        -- wrap_up/exit_session entirely. The wrap_up response carries a
        -- different rate_learning nudge, gated to the pr action rather than
        -- unconditional (see learnings.allium: WrapUpRateLearningNudge).
}

-- Anywhere an MCP tool takes a task id it also takes the name of one of the
-- task's agents.
--
-- `get_task`, `update_tasks`, `dispatch_task`, `wrap_up` and the rest resolve
-- the argument first: digits are an id, anything else is looked up as an agent
-- name, current or ended (agents.allium: AgentNameResolvesToTask). A word that
-- is no agent's name is the same NotFound (-32001) an unknown id gets. Results
-- still report the numeric id.
--
-- Task aliases are gone (agents.allium, step A5): an old alias no longer
-- resolves, and `update_tasks` has no `alias` field.
rule TaskRefResolvesToTaskId {
    when: McpToolCalled(task_ref)
    ensures: TaskResolved(task_ref)
}

rule UpdateTasksViaMcp {
    when: McpUpdateTasks(caller_identity, task_ids, fields)

    -- update_tasks is the only MCP tool that updates tasks. fields is the
    -- field set (status, sub_status, plan_path, title, description, repo_path,
    -- sort_order, url, url_type, tag, epic_id, base_branch, wrap_up_mode,
    -- model, auto_run_plan, phoenix), with the meaning UpdateTaskViaMcp gives it.

    requires: task_ids.count > 0

    -- Each id is handled exactly as UpdateTaskViaMcp (or MarkTaskDoneViaMcp
    -- for status = done) would handle it with that task and the same fields,
    -- independently of the others.
    ensures:
        for t in task_ids:
            McpUpdateTask(task: t, caller_identity: caller_identity, fields: fields)
    ensures: BulkUpdateReported(task_ids: task_ids)

    @guidance
        -- == One tool, one or many ids ==
        -- update_task used to exist beside this tool and did the same job for
        -- one id, with the same guards. Two tools for one job made a caller
        -- choose between them, so update_task is gone and update_tasks keeps
        -- the plural name: the many-id case is the one that needs the array.
        -- A caller updating one task passes task_ids: [id].
        --
        -- == Reported per task, except for one id ==
        -- With more than one id the call is not atomic. A failure on one task
        -- does not stop or roll back the others, and the response is a success
        -- even when some tasks fail. It has one line per id, in the order
        -- given: "Task N updated (fields)" or "Task N failed: <error
        -- message>", then a final "Updated X of Y tasks." A repeated id is
        -- handled once.
        --
        -- With exactly one id (after de-duplication) the reply is the old
        -- single-task reply: "Task N updated (fields)" on success, and on
        -- failure a real error response carrying the message, with no
        -- "failed:" line and no count. Reporting a lone failure inside a
        -- success response would hide it from a caller that checks for an
        -- error, and the count line says nothing about one task.
        --
        -- == Selection is explicit ids only ==
        -- There is no epic_id/recursive selection here. The caller lists the
        -- ids with list_tasks (epic_id, recursive, status, base_branch) and
        -- passes them. That keeps one way to select, and the caller's status
        -- filter on list_tasks decides whether done and running tasks are
        -- included. Every named task is updated, whatever its status.
        --
        -- An empty task_ids, a task_ids that is not an array of ids, or a
        -- task_id argument is rejected as invalid params (-32602) before any
        -- task is touched. A call with no field set is too: every task would
        -- fail the same way.
        -- Handled by handle_update_tasks in src/mcp/handlers/tasks/crud.rs.
}

rule CreateTaskViaMcp {
    when: McpCreateTask(caller_identity,
                        title, repo_path, epic_id, description?, plan?,
                        sort_order?, tag?, base_branch?,
                        wrap_up_mode?, model?, auto_run_plan?, phoenix?, schedule?, signal?)

    -- epic_id is REQUIRED and nullable. Required means the ARGUMENT must be
    -- present; nullable means the VALUE may be null. The three cases are:
    --   absent       — the call is refused (EveryTaskNamesItsEpicOrNull below)
    --   an integer   — that epic, which must exist
    --   null         — the task is deliberately standalone, with no epic
    -- There is no inheritance from the caller: a Task-kind caller's own epic
    -- is never consulted here, and the effective epic is exactly the argument.

    -- caller_identity is supplied by the dispatch HTTP transport, not by
    -- the tool arguments. It is one of:
    --   Task(task_id)   — a dispatched agent
    --   Session         — any non-dispatched session
    --
    -- The two identities reach the transport by different routes, and the
    -- headersHelper supplies only one of them:
    --   Task(id) — a fixed X-Caller-Task-Id header in the per-task MCP
    --     configuration the launcher writes for that agent (see
    --     AgentCarriesItsOwnCallerIdentity in dispatch.allium).
    --   Session — X-Caller-Kind: session, from the
    --     `dispatch caller-headers` headersHelper installed in Claude Code's
    --     user-global config (~/.claude.json) by `dispatch tui`'s startup
    --     configuration check (see startup.allium).
    --
    -- TheHelperAnswersSessionUnconditionally. The helper takes no reading of
    -- its surroundings and has no second answer to give: `session` is the
    -- whole of it. It once derived a task identity by inspecting its own
    -- working directory for a worktree path, which could not work — Claude
    -- Code runs a user-global helper from its own configuration directory,
    -- so the directory it inspected was never the agent's (see
    -- CallerIdentityDependsOnTheLaunch below). Keeping that derivation as an
    -- unreachable branch would leave two mechanisms answering "which task is
    -- this agent", one of them a dead end that the next investigation of a
    -- wrong caller identity would search first.

    -- A Task-kind caller must reference an existing task.
    requires:
        if caller_identity.kind = task: core/Task.exists(caller_identity.task_id)

    -- epic_id, when non-null, must refer to an existing epic.
    requires:
        if epic_id != null: core/Epic.exists(epic_id)

    ensures:
      let task = core/Task.created(
        title: title,
        description: description ?? "",
        repo_path: repo_path,
        status: backlog,
        sub_status: core/SubStatus.default_for(backlog),
        plan_path: plan,
        epic: route_target(epic_id, repo_path),
        tag: tag,
        sort_order: sort_order,
        base_branch: base_branch ?? detect_default_branch(repo_path),
        wrap_up_mode: wrap_up_mode,
        model: model,
        auto_run_plan: auto_run_plan ?? false,
        phoenix: phoenix ?? false,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
      )
      -- schedule as on update_tasks, applied in the same store write as the
      -- creation, so a refused cron creates no task.
      if schedule != null and schedule != "":
            schedules/ScheduleSet(task: task, cron: schedule)

    -- SignalMarksTheTaskForEveryFollower. When signal is true and the task
    -- lands in an epic, one core/UnseenTask row is written for every person
    -- holding a core/Subscription to that epic or to any of its ancestors
    -- (Epic.ancestor_chain), in the same store transaction as the task. The
    -- epic is the one the task LANDS in (route_target's), not the argument.
      if signal = true and task.epic != null:
            for s in core/Subscription.where(s => Epic.ancestor_chain(task.epic.id).contains(s.epic.id)):
                core/UnseenTask.created(subscriber: s.subscriber, task: task)

    -- signal is optional and defaults to false. Absent or false writes no
    -- row. With no epic there is no one following it, so signal is silently
    -- ignored and the task is created normally; the call is not refused.
    @guidance
        -- WHO IS SIGNALLED IS DECIDED AT CREATION. A person who subscribes to
        -- the epic afterwards is not marked for a task that already exists:
        -- the signal says "something new arrived while you were following",
        -- and a late follower was not following. Recorded because the
        -- alternative (derive "unseen" at read time from a follow date) was
        -- considered and rejected as a second clock to keep honest.
        --
        -- The caller's own subscription counts like anyone's. An agent that
        -- wants no mark for its operator omits signal.
        --
        -- Only create_task takes it as an argument. update_tasks does not.
        -- The one other creation path that turns it on is the reviews feed:
        -- a PR newly inserted into My Reviews is created with the signal on,
        -- marking the same followers this rule names (feeds.allium:
        -- NewMyReviewsTaskIsSignalled). The TUI form, quick dispatch and
        -- every other feed insert write no UnseenTask rows.

    -- BaseBranchIsResolvedNotAssumed. An omitted base_branch is resolved
    -- against the repository named by repo_path, by detect_default_branch --
    -- NOT defaulted to config.default_branch. This tool is the one creation
    -- path that had no human in front of it to notice a wrong answer: the TUI
    -- form shows its base branch in a picker the user can correct, and quick
    -- dispatch already detects (tasks.allium: CreateQuickTask). Assuming
    -- "main" here produced tasks in "master" repos that could not be
    -- dispatched at all -- `git worktree add` rejected the ref, and because
    -- the failing dispatch was usually the epic auto-dispatch chain, the only
    -- trace was a server-side warning. See
    -- dispatch.allium: DefaultBaseBranchIsDetectedNotAssumed.
    --
    -- The epic the task LANDS in is route_target's, not the argument
    -- verbatim: when epic_id names a grouped root (group_by_repo = true) the
    -- task is routed to the matching per-repo sub-epic, using repo_path as the
    -- routing key, and the sub-epic is created if it does not exist. Named
    -- here for the same reason UpdateTaskViaMcp and tasks.allium's CreateTask
    -- name it. Implemented by the store: its create_task reducer routes the
    -- epic named, in the same transaction as the insert, and recalculates
    -- the epic the task lands in (epics.allium's routing note above
    -- RegroupEpic). The service passes epic_id through unrouted.
    --
    -- EveryTaskNamesItsEpicOrNull. A call that omits epic_id entirely is
    -- REFUSED with a validation error, for BOTH caller kinds — Task(t) and
    -- Session alike. Nothing is created and no epic is guessed.
    --
    -- Why the argument is required rather than inherited. Silent inheritance
    -- propagates a blank: an epic-less task creates an epic-less child, which
    -- creates another. Measured on the real board, 93 of 209 omitted-epic_id
    -- calls produced a task with no epic, and every one of them had inherited
    -- correctly from a caller that itself had none — chains such as
    -- 4786 -> 4806 -> 4826 -> 4843, with no error raised on any surface.
    -- Requiring the argument stops the chain: a wrong answer now costs one
    -- task instead of a lineage.
    --
    -- Why that is cheap to ask. The value is already in front of the agent —
    -- the dispatch prompt states "This task is part of epic #N". Of the 236
    -- calls that did pass an explicit epic_id, exactly one named an epic that
    -- does not exist, and that case is already refused by the Epic.exists
    -- requirement above.
    --
    -- Why null is kept rather than forcing every task into an epic. A
    -- genuinely standalone task must stay expressible without inventing a
    -- catch-all epic to park it in. The point of the change is to force a
    -- DECISION, not to force an epic.
    --
    -- TheRefusalNamesTheLikelyAnswer. The validation error for a missing
    -- epic_id is actionable, not a bare "missing field". When the caller is
    -- Task(t) and that task has an epic, the message names that epic id as the
    -- likely answer, and states that null means deliberately standalone. This
    -- is why the requirement is enforced in the handler, where caller identity
    -- is known, rather than by the deserialization boundary's own
    -- missing-field error: that error cannot name the caller's epic.

    @guidance
        -- Dispatched agents never pass caller_task_id explicitly — the
        -- transport carries identity.
        --
        -- CallerIdentityDependsOnTheLaunch. A dispatched agent is identified
        -- because its LAUNCH said so, not because anything at the agent's end
        -- worked out which task it is. The headersHelper cannot: Claude Code
        -- runs a user-global helper from its own config directory, so a helper
        -- that tried to recognise a `.worktrees/<id>-<slug>` working directory
        -- would never see one. It therefore answers `session` and nothing else
        -- (TheHelperAnswersSessionUnconditionally above), which for a
        -- dispatched agent is indistinguishable, here, from a genuine
        -- non-dispatched session.
        --
        -- The degradation is silent on one of the two surfaces only. An agent
        -- launched without its per-task configuration gets no auto-scoping in
        -- ListTasksViaMcp, with no error — that half still stands, and
        -- ListTasksViaMcp cannot detect the difference. Here it no longer
        -- applies: create_task requires epic_id of every caller, so a
        -- misconfigured agent that omits it is REFUSED outright rather than
        -- quietly given no epic. What it loses is the refusal's help, not the
        -- refusal: TheRefusalNamesTheLikelyAnswer can only name the caller's
        -- epic when identity is Task(t), so a Session-identified agent is told
        -- the argument is required and nothing more.
        --
        -- The obligation still lives entirely on the dispatch side — see
        -- AgentCarriesItsOwnCallerIdentity in dispatch.allium.
        --
        -- The Axum middleware (src/mcp/middleware.rs) is an *extractor*,
        -- not a gatekeeper: it parses the two headers and attaches a
        -- Result<CallerIdentity, IdentityError> to request extensions
        -- without ever short-circuiting the request. This is required
        -- because the middleware runs before the JSON-RPC body is
        -- parsed and therefore has no request id to echo back — and a
        -- JSON-RPC response with `id: null` is rejected by strict MCP
        -- clients (Claude Code), which would abort the session during
        -- the handshake.
        --
        -- Identity-free methods (`initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list`,
        -- notifications) succeed regardless of header presence.
        -- Identity-consuming tool calls (everything dispatched through
        -- `tools/call`) reject missing or malformed identity at the
        -- handler boundary with JSON-RPC -32600, echoing back the
        -- request's actual id.
        -- An unknown caller-task is a not-found condition, not a
        -- validation one: the shared caller-task lookup (fetch_caller_task
        -- in src/mcp/handlers/types.rs) rejects it with -32001, the same
        -- code every ServiceError::NotFound maps to.
        -- An unknown epic_id is a not-found condition, like an unknown
        -- caller-task above, and returns -32001 for the same reason: the epic
        -- named is not there, which is not the same answer as the argument
        -- being malformed. require_epic_accepting_work (src/service/epics.rs)
        -- raises it, so create_task and the
        -- reassign path in MoveTaskToEpic give one answer to one operator
        -- mistake rather than -32602 and -32001 by entry point. It sits in the
        -- service rather than the handler so the TUI create path is held to it
        -- too. Left to the SQLite foreign key instead, as it once was, it
        -- surfaced as a -32603 Internal whose text named neither the epic nor
        -- the argument.
        -- Unlike the TUI CreateTask rule, this rule does not fire
        -- RecordRepoPath — MCP task creation skips repo path persistence.
        -- repo_path is expanded through expand_tilde before being
        -- stored (so "~/proj" becomes "$HOME/proj"), matching
        -- UpdateTaskViaMcp.
}

rule GetTaskViaMcp {
    when: McpGetTask(task_id)

    requires: core/Task.exists(task_id)

    -- Read-only. Returns the task detail view for task_id; no state changes.
    ensures: TaskRetrieved(task_id)

    @guidance
        -- get_task takes only task_id and is the tool a dispatched agent uses
        -- to read its own task (title, description, plan, tag, wrap_up_mode).
        -- Caller identity is not consulted: any caller may read any task, and
        -- there is no auto-scoping or self-exclusion as in ListTasksViaMcp.
        -- Detail lines, in render order (lines in [brackets] appear only when
        -- the field is set):
        --   Task <id>: <title>, Status, Repo, Description, Sub-status,
        --   [Epic (as "<title> (#<id>)", or "#<id>" when the title cannot be
        --   resolved)], [Tag], [Plan], [<url label>: <url> — the typed label
        --   derived from url.url_type], [Worktree], Base branch,
        --   [Tmux window], [Dispatch request], [Sort order], [Verify command],
        --   [Wrap-up mode], Model, [Phoenix], [Schedule], [Schedule error],
        --   Created, Updated.
        -- Model always renders, as the alias the agent launches on: task.model,
        -- or "sonnet (default)" when it is null. A missing line would read as
        -- "no model applies", and a null model is a concrete one
        -- (core/TaskModel).
        -- [Dispatch request] is not a Task field: it is the task's most recent
        -- store-split.allium DispatchRequest that this board's subscription
        -- holds, rendered as its id, state and, when failed, its reason.
        -- Omitted when there is none. It is how a dispatch_task caller whose
        -- wait ended while the request was still pending learns how it ended.
        -- [Phoenix] renders only when the flag is true, and says the task will
        -- come back — so an agent wrapping up knows it is finishing THIS run of
        -- a recurring task, not the task itself, and can leave notes for its
        -- successor in the description rather than only in the commit.
        -- [Schedule] renders when task.schedule is set, as the cron string
        -- marked UTC and, when next_due is set, the next due time.
        -- [Schedule error] renders task.schedule_error when set.
        -- Not surfaced at all: labels, external_id, auto_run_plan, origin, and
        -- the hook timestamps (last_pre_tool_use_at, last_notification_at,
        -- pr_learnings_gate_shown_at).
        --
        -- [Verify command] is not a Task field: it is task.repo_path resolved
        -- against core/SavedRepoPath.verify_command.
        -- Omitted when no verify_command is set for the repo. The line is how
        -- the agent learns the command it runs itself, while it works and
        -- before wrap_up (AgentsRunTheVerifyCommand); the board never runs it. See also ThePromptCarriesNoVerifyCommand in
        -- dispatch-prompt.allium.
        -- An unknown task_id is a not-found condition, not a validation one:
        -- ServiceError::NotFound surfaces as the -32001 not-found code, the
        -- same mapping every other NotFound uses (see CreateTaskViaMcp).
        --
        -- == The tool description is one sentence ==
        -- The description says, in one short sentence, that get_task returns
        -- a task's details. The format is shown by what it returns: the
        -- response is labelled "Label: value" lines, and a reader that has the
        -- response in front of it needs no prose describing it. It used to
        -- name the format, the absent-line-means-unset convention and the
        -- three lines a wrapping-up agent reads. Naming those lines belongs to
        -- the /wrap-up skill, the caller that reads them, not to the tool.
        -- Handled by handle_get_task in src/mcp/handlers/tasks/crud.rs.
        --
        -- == The current agent ==
        -- A task with a current agent (agents.allium: AgentIdentity) gets one
        -- line, "Agent: <name> (live)" or "Agent: <name> (paused)", straight
        -- after the Description line: live when the agent holds a tmux window,
        -- paused when it does not. A task with no current agent has no such
        -- line, as an unset field has none. An ended agent is never shown: it
        -- still resolves as a task_ref (TaskRefResolvesToTaskId), but it is not
        -- the one working the task.
}

rule ListTasksViaMcp {
    when: McpListTasks(caller_identity,
                       epic_id?, repo_paths?, status?, recursive?, base_branch?,
                       query?, limit?)

    -- A limit of zero is meaningless: rejected as invalid params (-32602).
    requires:
        if limit != null: limit >= 1

    -- caller_identity comes from the HTTP transport (see CreateTaskViaMcp).

    ensures: TasksQueried(
        caller_identity: caller_identity,
        epic_id: epic_id,
        repo_paths: repo_paths,
        status: status,
        recursive: recursive,
        base_branch: base_branch,
        query: query,
        limit: limit
    )

    @guidance
        -- == each row names the current agent ==
        -- A row for a task with a current agent carries " agent:<name> (live)"
        -- or " agent:<name> (paused)" after the title, with the same
        -- meaning as get_task's Agent line. A task with none shows nothing.
        -- == query is a text search over title and description ==
        -- query, when set, keeps only tasks that match it. It is split on
        -- whitespace into words; a task matches when EVERY word is a
        -- case-insensitive substring of its title or its description (a word
        -- may match in either field, different words in different fields).
        -- Word order does not matter. A blank query (empty or only
        -- whitespace) has no words and is the same as omitting it. It is ANDed
        -- with every other filter and it is not a scope filter: it does not
        -- disable auto-scoping, so an agent searching with a query still
        -- searches only its own epic unless it passes epic_id/repo_paths.
        -- The same matching rule serves list_epics (ListEpicsViaMcp in
        -- epics.allium). Substring, not whole-word: "dispatch" matches
        -- "redispatch". No ranking: matches come back in the usual list order.
        -- When nothing matches the reply is the usual "No tasks found".
        -- == limit caps the rows, with no default ==
        -- limit is optional and has NO default: without it the list is
        -- complete, as before. A default would silently truncate the plain
        -- list that callers rely on being complete (a bulk change would miss
        -- tasks without knowing). With limit set, the first `limit` rows in
        -- the usual order are returned after every filter has been applied.
        -- When rows were cut, a final line says so:
        --   "Showing <limit> of <total>. Narrow your query or filters."
        -- When nothing is cut there is no such line. The same rule serves
        -- list_epics (ListEpicsViaMcp in epics.allium).
        --
        -- == recursive widens the epic scope to the whole subtree ==
        -- recursive (default false) applies to the epic scope in effect,
        -- explicit or auto-derived (below): with it, a task matches when its
        -- epic is the scope epic or any descendant of it (core/Epic
        -- parent_epic_id chain), not only the scope epic itself. Without it
        -- the walk had to be done by hand, one get_epic per guessed id, and a
        -- missed sub-epic silently left its tasks out of a bulk change.
        -- recursive = true with no epic scope in effect (a session caller with
        -- no epic_id, or an agent with no epic that passed repo_paths) is
        -- rejected as invalid params (-32602): the board-wide list is already
        -- every task, so the flag would mean nothing.
        --
        -- == base_branch filters by exact match and every row shows it ==
        -- base_branch, when set, keeps only tasks whose base_branch equals it
        -- exactly. It is not a scope filter: it does not disable auto-scoping.
        -- Every output row carries " | Base: <base_branch>", so a caller can
        -- see which tasks need a change before making it.
        --
        -- When the caller is Task(t) and none of epic_id/repo_paths
        -- are set, auto-derive scope from the caller:
        --   caller.epic_id != null → filter by epic_id = caller.epic_id
        -- Explicit scope filters always win over auto-derived scope.
        -- When the caller is Task(t) alongside an explicit scope filter, the
        -- caller's task is still excluded from results but no additional
        -- scope is derived.
        -- When the caller is Session, no auto-scope and no self-exclusion.
        --
        -- This auto-scoping is UNCHANGED by EveryTaskNamesItsEpicOrNull in
        -- CreateTaskViaMcp. Inheriting the caller's epic was removed from
        -- create_task only, where it silently propagated a blank into newly
        -- created work. Here nothing is created: the caller's epic narrows a
        -- read that would otherwise return the whole board, an omitted
        -- epic_id simply means "no narrowing", and a wrong guess costs the
        -- caller one over-broad list rather than a mis-parented task.
        -- Output line per task: title, status/sub_status, tag, epic, then
        --   | Base: <base_branch>
        --   | <label>: <url.url>  (omitted when url is null), where <label>
        --     is the typed label derived from url.url_type: "PR #N" / "Issue #N"
        --     / "Security Alert" / "Link"
        --   | Goal: <plan goal>  (from **Goal:** line in plan file; falls back to
        --                         description preview ≤200 chars when plan absent/unreadable)
        --
        -- == The tool description names every url label, not just PR ==
        -- The url line is typed, so three of the four labels are not "PR". The
        -- description names all four rather than describing the output as a PR
        -- url, which is what it said while the url field was PR-only: a caller
        -- who scans for the word "PR" on a security-alert or issue task finds
        -- nothing and concludes the task carries no link.
        -- Implemented in handle_list_tasks (src/mcp/handlers/tasks/crud.rs).
}

rule QueryUsageViaMcp {
    when: McpQueryUsage(category?, actor?, since?)

    -- category and actor, when supplied, are validated against their
    -- enums; an unrecognised value is rejected rather than silently
    -- yielding an empty result.
    requires:
        if category != null: usage_category_is_valid(category)
    requires:
        if actor != null: usage_actor_is_valid(actor)

    ensures: FeatureUsageQueried(
        category: category,
        actor: actor,
        since: since
    )

    @guidance
        -- query_usage returns aggregated feature-usage counts grouped by
        -- category + action (with detail, actor, total count and the most
        -- recent use timestamp per group). Filters are optional:
        --   category: "keybinding" | "mcp_tool" (enum-validated)
        --   actor:    enum-validated string
        --   since:    ISO-8601 datetime; rows older than this are excluded
        -- Invalid category/actor strings reject with JSON-RPC -32602; a
        -- malformed `since` likewise rejects with -32602.
        -- These are feature-usage counters (which keybindings and MCP tools
        -- are exercised), NOT token counts. Per-call usage recording fires
        -- on every tools/call request.
        -- Handled by handle_query_usage in src/mcp/handlers/tasks/crud.rs.
}

rule DispatchTaskViaMcp {
    when: McpDispatchTask(task)

    requires: task.status = backlog

    -- No mode: the module derives it from the task (store-split.allium:
    -- RequestDispatch), by the same rule dispatch.allium's two entry rules
    -- encode. The request names this board's host, so this board provisions.
    ensures: DispatchRequested(task: task, requester: core/local_host(), origin: mcp)

    @guidance
        -- Returns an error if task.status != backlog.
        -- One request covers both has_plan = true (with-plan prompt) and
        -- has_plan = false (spec-first prompt) variants — the prompt builder
        -- branches internally — and a plan-less research-tagged task gets the
        -- research mode from the module's derivation.
        -- dispatch_task is the only remaining MCP dispatch tool: it stays the
        -- explicit "dispatch this specific task" call, while epic chaining is
        -- automatic on a session close that takes effect
        -- (AutoDispatchNextSubtask in epics.allium, keyed on the SessionClosed
        -- that only a close which took effect emits).
        --
        -- == A request, then a capped wait (store-split M4) ==
        -- The call writes a dispatch request (store-split.allium:
        -- RequestDispatch), which is also the claim: the backlog -> running
        -- transition is taken conditionally in the same transaction, so a
        -- concurrent chain or TUI dispatch either loses to this call or beats
        -- it outright, and the task is never provisioned twice
        -- (DispatchClaimExclusive in dispatch.allium). A refused request is
        -- reported as the same "not in backlog" error a task that was already
        -- running would produce, naming the status the task actually holds
        -- now; a task whose worktree is on another machine, or that still has
        -- an open request, is refused with that reason instead.
        --
        -- The call then waits on the request row, for at most
        -- config.dispatch_task_wait_cap, and answers by the row's state:
        --   - dispatched: the worktree path and tmux window, and whether the
        --     worktree was created or reused (below) — the same success
        --     shape as before M4.
        --   - failed: an error carrying the row's reason. The claim has
        --     already been released by the module (store-split.allium:
        --     ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest), so the task is left in backlog
        --     exactly as it was before the call, unless it was moved on
        --     meanwhile. The call itself releases nothing.
        --   - still requested or provisioning when the cap passes: NOT an
        --     error. The response gives the request id and its state, and
        --     says that get_task shows how the request ends
        --     (GetTaskViaMcp's [Dispatch request] line). The dispatch carries
        --     on; nothing is cancelled when the wait ends.
        -- Because a request always names the host that made it, and the MCP
        -- endpoint runs in this board, the request this call writes is always
        -- provisioned by this same board; there is no "another machine"
        -- answer.
        --
        -- == The success text names which provisioning path ran ==
        -- Provisioning has two paths (DispatchTask in dispatch.allium): a
        -- worktree directory that does not exist yet is CREATED by branching
        -- from a resolved start point, and one that already exists is REUSED —
        -- `git worktree add` is skipped, so the directory keeps its branch, its
        -- commits and its uncommitted changes, and the agent starts in them.
        -- Which ref the fresh path branches from is dispatch.allium's
        -- start-point table's business, not this rule's: it is usually the
        -- task's base branch, but a review task carrying a PR url branches from
        -- the PR's head instead, and an origin that has no such ref falls back
        -- to the local branch. What matters here is only that one path branches
        -- and the other does not.
        --
        -- The success text says which of the two happened, naming the worktree
        -- either as created or as reused. This is not decoration. It is the
        -- only place a caller learns, from the call itself, that a re-dispatch
        -- did not throw the previous session's work away — and a caller who is
        -- told cannot mistake a resume for a fresh start. Reporting one wording
        -- for both paths would leave the caller to infer the difference from
        -- the tool description, which is exactly the inference that was
        -- measured going wrong: an orchestrating agent read "creates a
        -- worktree", concluded that nine crashed tasks' unlanded commits and
        -- dirty trees were unreachable, and wrote cherry-pick-and-`git apply`
        -- salvage tasks for work that a plain re-dispatch would have resumed
        -- in place.
        --
        -- The distinction is drawn where provisioning already establishes it,
        -- and is reported rather than re-derived. Only provisioning can tell
        -- the two apart: it measures the directory BEFORE acting, and by the
        -- time the dispatch has returned the directory exists either way, so a
        -- later look at it answers a different question. So provisioning
        -- reports it with the dispatched report, the module records it on the
        -- request row (store-split.allium: DispatchRequest.reused_worktree,
        -- HostReportsDispatched), and this call reads it from the row it
        -- waited on.
        --
        -- == The tool description advertises both paths and the recovery move ==
        -- The description a caller reads before calling states both paths, that
        -- a reused worktree keeps its branch, commits and uncommitted changes,
        -- and the move those two facts add up to: a crashed or stalled task is
        -- resumed in place by setting it back to backlog with update_tasks and
        -- dispatching it again. It also tells the caller to say in the task
        -- what the worktree already holds, because the resumed agent is a new
        -- agent and carries none of the crashed session's context. And it
        -- says what the call returns when provisioning outlasts the wait: the
        -- request id and its state rather than the worktree. What to do then
        -- is told in that response, not in the description.
        --
        -- This description is the ONE place the recovery move is told.
        -- update_tasks' `status` argument carried a copy for a while, on the
        -- grounds that backlog is where a caller meets the move; it no longer
        -- does. A caller resuming a task is about to call dispatch_task, so
        -- that is where it reads the story, and a second copy was a second
        -- wording to keep in step by hand.
}

rule ResumeTaskViaMcp {
    when: McpResumeTask(task, message?)

    -- The refusals, each an error naming the reason. The board's own view of
    -- the task decides, the same view Space acts on.
    requires: task.status in {running, review, done}
    requires: task.worktree != null
    requires: current_agent(task).tmux_window = null
    requires: task.is_locally_owned
    -- The worktree the row names must still be a directory on this machine:
    -- a cleaned-up worktree leaves the row but no conversation to continue.
    requires: path_exists_on_disk(task.worktree)

    -- The same rule the TUI's Space key raises, not a second launch path.
    ensures: ResumeTask(task: task, message: message)

    @guidance
        -- resume_task wakes a detached (paused) agent WITH its context. It
        -- reattaches the task's worktree and starts `claude --continue`, so
        -- the agent's previous conversation is restored, and puts the task
        -- back to running. The older route — update_tasks status=backlog,
        -- then dispatch_task — starts a NEW agent in the reused worktree
        -- with none of that context (DispatchTaskViaMcp).
        --
        -- == Arguments ==
        -- task_id (a task id or agent name, like every task tool) and an
        -- optional message, delivered as the resumed session's first input
        -- (dispatch.allium: ResumeTask). The message is the one place to say
        -- why the agent was woken; the task description is not rewritten.
        --
        -- == Errors ==
        -- Each refused precondition is an error that says what is wrong in
        -- plain words: the task has no worktree (it never ran, so there is
        -- no session to resume; dispatch it); its status is not running,
        -- review or done (backlog tasks are dispatched, not resumed); an agent
        -- is already attached to it (ListAgents/SendMessage reach it); its
        -- worktree is on another machine; its worktree directory is gone from
        -- disk (the agent is no longer there). "No resumable session" is the
        -- umbrella: the tool never starts a fresh agent as a fallback.
        --
        -- == Answer ==
        -- The call hands the resume to the board and answers that it was
        -- requested, naming the task and its tmux window. It does not wait for
        -- the session to finish starting. A launch that fails after the hand-off
        -- (tmux or claude missing) shows in the board's status line, and
        -- get_task shows the task still detached.
        --
        -- == Not for crashed worktrees ==
        -- A task whose worktree is gone cannot resume; it is re-dispatched
        -- from backlog (DispatchTaskViaMcp). The tool description and the
        -- agent-facing skills say resume_task first, and dispatch_task only
        -- when there is no session to resume.
}

-- Inter-agent messaging no longer has a dispatch MCP tool. It was
-- `send_message` (from_task_id, to_task_id, body) → a message file under the
-- target's worktree + a `tmux send-keys` nudge — replaced (task #4098) after
-- reproducing that the nudge could land on a permission prompt or
-- plan-mode/elicitation dialog instead of the agent's chat input, silently
-- answering it rather than being read. See
-- docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-15-send-message-delivery-hardening-design.md
-- for that reproduction.
--
-- Agents now use Claude Code's own native `ListAgents`/`SendMessage` tools
-- directly, addressed by the `task-<id>` session name dispatch assigns at
-- launch (`dispatch.allium`'s `DispatchTask`/`ResumeTask`). Delivery is
-- queued and read between tool calls — it cannot land on a dialog, because
-- it never touches the pane's raw stdin. Dispatch never sits in the call
-- path; it only observes the sender's own tool call via the existing Claude
-- Code hook pipeline to drive the TUI flash and reconstruct an audit entry —
-- see `HookPeerMessageSent` in `agent-health.allium`.

-- == CLI surface ==

rule AttachPlanViaCli {
    when: CliAttachPlan(task, path)

    requires: task != null

    ensures: task.plan_path = path

    @guidance
        -- CLI command: `dispatch plan <id> <path>` attaches a plan file to
        -- an existing task by writing task.plan_path directly via
        -- db.patch_task. No status transition occurs; the plan attachment
        -- is decoupled from dispatch.
        -- Implementation: src/main.rs Commands::Plan match arm.
        -- Used by the /queue-plan slash command (and operators) to stage
        -- a plan onto a task before dispatching it.
}

-- @guidance: Other CLI subcommands (operator/diagnostic surfaces; not
-- modeled as separate rules because they do not mutate task domain state
-- beyond what existing rules already cover):
--   • `dispatch tui [--port]` — the only entry point, launching the TUI
--     (src/main.rs Commands::Tui). --port also reads the DISPATCH_PORT env
--     var and defaults to core/config.mcp_port. It obtains its own tmux
--     session and offers to write the Claude Code MCP configuration before
--     the board draws — see startup.allium, and CreateTaskViaMcp guidance for
--     the headersHelper that configuration installs. There is no separate
--     setup command. The other subcommands that reach the store default to
--     http://127.0.0.1:3000 (startup.allium's config.managed_store_address)
--     when no store is named, and start, adopt or stop nothing: only the
--     board manages the local store.
--   • `dispatch uninstall [--yes] [--purge]` — destructive: removes the
--     dispatch MCP configuration from Claude Code; with `--purge` also
--     deletes this machine's host file (host.allium: HostFile) and the log
--     file from the data directory, and the directory itself if that leaves
--     it empty (src/main.rs Commands::Uninstall). The confirmation it asks
--     first says that this machine's identity will be forgotten — a later
--     launch mints a new one, and tasks stamped with the old host id stop
--     being this machine's — and states no task count: tasks live in the
--     store, which a purge does not touch, and counting them would mean
--     opening a database (storage.allium: StoreInUseNeverOpensSqlite). It
--     never opens, reads or deletes a leftover `tasks.db`, `-wal` or `-shm`
--     (storage.allium: CodeNeverTouchesLegacyDatabase); those are the
--     operator's to delete by hand.
--     Operator-only — never invoked by agents or hooks.
--   • `dispatch repo set-verify <path> <command>` /
--     `dispatch repo clear-verify <path>` / `dispatch repo list` — operator
--     equivalents of SetVerifyCommandViaMcp, writing the same
--     core/SavedRepoPath.verify_command. set-verify creates the path entry
--     when it does not exist yet.
--   • `dispatch prune-repo-paths` — drops the core/SavedRepoPath rows THIS host
--     saved whose path no longer exists on its filesystem; other hosts' rows
--     are left alone (cli.allium: PruneRepoPathsViaCli). Operator-only housekeeping.
--   • `dispatch hook <id> <kind> [--kind K]` — see HookNotification in agent-health.allium.
--   • `dispatch pr-gate <id>` — see PrLearningsGate in pr-workflow.allium.
--   • `dispatch caller-headers` — prints the session identity header and
--     exits 0, always. Reads nothing: not its arguments, not its working
--     directory, not the database. See CreateTaskViaMcp guidance.
--   • `dispatch verify-feed <command>` — see VerifyFeed in feeds.allium.
--
-- There is deliberately NO CLI surface for listing tasks or changing a
-- task's status. The `dispatch list` and `dispatch update` subcommands were
-- removed once the installed Claude Code hooks moved to the dedicated
-- `hook-*` subcommands and agents moved to the MCP tools; the board and the
-- MCP tools are the only surfaces for reading and mutating task state.

-- == MCP Wrap-Up and Exit Tools ==

rule WrapUpViaMcp {
    when: McpWrapUp(task, action)

    requires: task.status = running or task.status = review
    requires: task.worktree != null
    -- A review task does not end by being wrapped up (ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp).
    -- The code gates on the core/TaskTag::is_review predicate rather than on
    -- these three literals, so the clause below is the narrower statement.
    -- What keeps them in step is a test, not this comment: the TaskTag model
    -- test that derives the review set from TaskTag::ALL fails on a new review
    -- tag and sends whoever added it here (core.allium: "THE REVIEW SET").
    requires: task.tag not in {pr_review, dependabot, release_please}
    -- WrapUpRefusesUnfinishedWork, below: the worktree is committed. Checked
    -- before the action runs, and before any request is written, so a refusal
    -- has no side effect and issues no token. It is an observation of the
    -- worktree made by running a subprocess, so it is named by the guarantee
    -- rather than modelled as a field: `worktree_is_committed` is "git status
    -- --porcelain prints nothing" (untracked files count). The repository's
    -- verify command is NOT checked here: the agent runs it itself
    -- (AgentsRunTheVerifyCommand).
    requires: worktree_is_committed(task.worktree)

    -- For rebase, the worktree path names a branch (the rebase needs one).
    -- Checked here, after the commit check above and before any request is
    -- written, so an underivable branch is refused as a bad request
    -- ("Cannot derive branch from worktree: <path>", INVALID_PARAMS) rather
    -- than reported as a failed rebase. done and pr need no branch.
    requires: action = rebase implies worktree_names_a_branch(task.worktree)

    -- All three actions leave task.status unchanged (still running/review) and
    -- only issue an exit token whose request row records which action was
    -- requested. The terminal mutation (Done, or Review + pr-typed url) is
    -- deferred to the single ExitSessionViaMcp call. pr_url is no longer a
    -- wrap_up param — it travels with exit_session instead.
    --
    -- Since store-split M5 the tool ASKS: one request_wrap_up call naming
    -- this board's host (store-split.allium: RequestWrapUp). For rebase the
    -- host's board then runs the rebase (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase);
    -- for done and pr the module writes the row
    -- already done.
    ensures: WrapUpRequested(task: task, host: core/local_host(), action: action)

    @guidance
        -- wrap_up is the mandatory step before exit_session for ALL three
        -- actions (rebase, done, pr). The exit token is the id of the done
        -- wrap_up request row (store-split.allium, W3), which records the
        -- action; the response text carries it, and the agent must pass it
        -- back to exit_session.
        -- action="rebase" is the only action with host work: the rebase /
        -- fast-forward via finish_task, which performs no
        -- session teardown, so the session stays alive until exit_session.
        -- action="done" and action="pr" perform no task-status
        -- or url mutation at wrap_up time — done no longer flips to Done, and
        -- pr no longer sets task.url or moves the task to review. Those
        -- mutations are applied later, atomically, by ExitSessionViaMcp. This
        -- removes the old asymmetry where done/pr mutated status inside
        -- wrap_up while rebase deferred to exit_session.
        --
        -- == The request, then a wait (store-split M5) ==
        -- The tool checks the request first, in today's order: wrappable
        -- (running or review, a worktree, ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp), then
        -- WrapUpRefusesUnfinishedWork (commit), then for rebase
        -- the branch. Only then does it call request_wrap_up. It subscribes to the request rows' changes BEFORE
        -- writing, so no change to the row is missed, and answers:
        --   - refused by the module (not running or review, no worktree, a
        --     review-tagged task, the worktree on another machine, an open
        --     session request already exists): an INVALID_PARAMS error
        --     carrying the module's reason;
        --     nothing was written.
        --   - done or pr: the row is written done in the same call, so the
        --     tool answers at once with the success text below, carrying the
        --     row's id as the token.
        --   - rebase: the tool WAITS on the row, waking on the subscription's
        --     change signal, until it finishes, for at most its deadline plus
        --     the backstop grace (store-split.allium:
        --     config.wrap_up_rebase_timeout,
        --     config.session_request_backstop_grace), by which time the host
        --     or the backstop has finished it. done: the rebase success text
        --     with the row's id as the token. failed: an INTERNAL_ERROR
        --     "wrap_up failed: <reason>" — a conflict's reason names the
        --     conflicted files, and the module has set the conflict
        --     sub_status; "timed out", "deadline passed", "host restarted"
        --     and a host-side internal error (the rebase worker died) read
        --     the same way, and the agent calls wrap_up again once the cause
        --     is fixed. Still open when the wait ends (the
        --     store unreachable): an INTERNAL_ERROR naming the request id
        --     and saying no token was issued and to call wrap_up again; a
        --     second request is refused while the first is still open.
        -- The success texts are today's: the action, that the session is NOT
        -- yet closed, "Exit token: <id>" with the
        -- exit_session instruction naming the action (and pr_url for pr),
        -- the rate_learning nudge for pr (learnings.allium:
        -- WrapUpRateLearningNudge), "No git operations performed." for done,
        -- and for rebase the warning that without exit_session the window
        -- stays alive, plus, when the rebase moved the worktree's HEAD and the
        -- repo has a verify command, the line from AgentsRunTheVerifyCommand.
        -- The /wrap-up skill runs the /retro skill before its commit step,
        -- ahead of wrap_up, so any agent-context fix retro makes is committed
        -- with the session's work rather than stranded after the rebase.
        -- For the pr path the agent has already run git push and
        -- gh pr create by this point, and passes the resulting URL to
        -- exit_session (not to wrap_up).
        -- Handled by handle_wrap_up in src/mcp/handlers/tasks/wrap_up.rs.

    @guarantee TheNextCallIsNamedJustInTime
        -- The instruction to call exit_session next, as the session's final
        -- action, is stated in wrap_up's success RESPONSE — the moment it
        -- applies, with the token it needs beside it — and by one short clause
        -- in the /wrap-up skill. It is stated nowhere else.
        --
        -- The wrap_up and exit_session tool DESCRIPTIONS say only what each
        -- tool does: wrap_up performs the chosen action and returns an exit
        -- token; exit_session closes the session with that token and, when the
        -- task's epic auto-dispatches, starts the epic's next backlog subtask.
        -- Neither carries MUST, "never stop after", or a restatement of the
        -- other's role. Those words reach every agent on every tools/list, on
        -- sessions that never wrap up, and repeating an instruction three
        -- times in capitals reads as distrust rather than as information.
        --
        -- The /wrap-up skill no longer carries a section telling the agent not
        -- to dispatch the epic's next subtask itself. exit_session owns that
        -- behaviour and its description says it happens; there is no tool an
        -- agent could misuse to do it anyway.

    @guarantee WrapUpRefusesUnfinishedWork
        -- Skipping the commit is how agents went idle with work stranded on
        -- an uncommitted tree, so the step a tool can check cheaply is
        -- enforced by the tool, not by the /wrap-up skill's prose.
        --
        -- Commit. `git status --porcelain` in the task's worktree must be
        -- empty, untracked files included. A status that cannot be read (git
        -- fails or exits non-zero) refuses too: the check fails closed.
        -- Otherwise wrap_up is refused, naming the uncommitted paths (at most
        -- a short list, then a count) and saying to commit them and call
        -- wrap_up again. This also makes the retro/commit ORDER hold in the
        -- one way observable from here: a retro fix left uncommitted blocks
        -- the call instead of being stranded after the rebase.
        --
        -- Refused wrap-ups are INVALID_PARAMS errors, as for a backlog task. A
        -- dirty tree changes no task state.
        --
        -- What is NOT enforced: that /retro ran, and that the verify command
        -- passed (AgentsRunTheVerifyCommand). A retro leaves no trace the
        -- tool can read (it may change nothing), so requiring it would need a
        -- marker the agent sets itself, which is the same honour system as the
        -- prose. It stays one line in the skill; only its fixes are held to the
        -- commit check above. Choosing the action first stays in the skill for
        -- the same reason: it is a question for the user, not a state of the
        -- repository.

    @guarantee AgentsRunTheVerifyCommand
        -- The board never runs a repository's verify command. The agent runs
        -- it, in its own worktree, with its own shell: a long test run is
        -- then not bounded by the agent's tool-call timeout, the board spends
        -- no time or memory on it, and there is no board-side verify state
        -- (no timeout, no wait cap, no outcome memory, no stdin or sccache
        -- handling for it). wrap_up does not check that the command passed
        -- and issues a token whether or not it did: the board trusts the
        -- agent (task #81924).
        --
        -- The command reaches the agent through get_task's "Verify command"
        -- line (GetTaskViaMcp), and the /wrap-up skill has a step that sends
        -- the agent to run it, fix failures and commit BEFORE it calls
        -- wrap_up. On the pr path that step comes before the push.
        --
        -- One case is named just in time in wrap_up's rebase response: when
        -- the rebase moved the worktree's HEAD (the base branch had commits
        -- the branch lacked, so the branch was rewritten on top of them) and
        -- the task's repo has a verify command, the success text adds a line
        -- naming the command and telling the agent to re-run it before
        -- exit_session. A rebase that left HEAD where it was, a done or pr
        -- action, and a repo with no verify command add no such line. The
        -- board does not run the command or check the result.
        --
        -- When the verify command fails for a reason the agent did not
        -- cause, including a broken base branch, no fixed procedure is
        -- specified. The repository's CLAUDE.md tells agents to coordinate
        -- with other agents and the board (look for a task already fixing
        -- it, otherwise propose or create one) rather than wrapping up past a
        -- red result alone.

    @guarantee ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp
        -- A review-tagged task is refused, whatever its status and whatever
        -- action is asked for. Such a task has no wrap-up to perform: it ends
        -- when its PR merges, or when the agent hands it back to the user and
        -- the user takes it over. Nothing is committed, rebased, or PR'd on
        -- its behalf, so every one of the three actions would be a no-op that
        -- still spent an exit token.
        --
        -- What cleans it up is the FEED's stale pass (feeds.allium:
        -- AppendOnlyFeed's counterpart — a card whose PR has merged stops
        -- being emitted and is deleted on the next poll), not
        -- pr-workflow.allium's PollPrStatus. PollPrStatus filters on
        -- status = review with a pr-typed url, and a review task never
        -- reaches that pair: the only rule that sets it is the terminal
        -- ExitSessionViaMcp patch, which this guarantee makes unreachable.
        -- So a review task lives its whole life in running.
        --
        -- That also bounds the claim. A feed-created review task is cleaned
        -- up; a review task dispatched by hand is not, and after this refusal
        -- nothing the agent can call releases its worktree or tmux window —
        -- the user closes it from the TUI. Behaviour is unchanged from the
        -- prompt line this replaced, which forbade wrap-up just as flatly,
        -- but the limit is worth naming rather than implying an auto-clean
        -- that only some of these tasks get.
        --
        -- This was a prompt instruction before it was a guard. Both review
        -- runbooks opened with "do not call /wrap-up", which is a rule
        -- stated where it cannot be enforced: an agent that ignores the
        -- line reaches a tool that accepts the call. Refusing here is what
        -- lets the line go, and it also covers the agents the line never
        -- reached — a review task the user dispatched by hand, or one the
        -- agent resumed in a later session.
        --
        -- The refusal names the tag as the reason, says a review task ends
        -- at its PR rather than at wrap-up, and names the one way out: a
        -- review task that has genuinely become code work is retagged
        -- (update_tasks with a non-review tag) and wrapped up as that. The
        -- escape hatch is stated because the alternative is an agent that
        -- has done real work and is told only "no" — it would leave the
        -- work uncommitted rather than infer the retag.
        --
        -- The rule lives in that refusal message, not in wrap_up's tool
        -- description. A refused caller is certain to read the error; every
        -- other caller reads the description on every tools/list and has no
        -- use for the rule. The description may say in passing that a
        -- review-tagged task is refused, and no more — the reason and the
        -- retag escape hatch are the message's.
        --
        -- Refusing wrap_up leaves exit_session unreachable for these tasks
        -- (ExitSessionViaMcp requires a token wrap_up mints), which is the
        -- intended shape: a review agent stops when it has presented its
        -- findings, exactly as build_prompt's review runbooks describe.
        --
        -- Since store-split M5 the refusal is the MODULE's, by decision with
        -- the user: request_wrap_up refuses a review-tagged task (pr_review,
        -- dependabot or release_please; store-split.allium: RequestWrapUp,
        -- W2), so no client can wrap one
        -- up. The tool may still check it first, as an early refusal before
        -- the commit check, with the same wording.
        --
        -- The refusal is also REPORTED by GetTaskViaMcp, on a "Wrap-up:
        -- refused — …" line carrying the same sentence. wrap_up is the last
        -- call of the /wrap-up skill's sequence and get_task is its second,
        -- so a refusal delivered only at the tool lets an agent run a retro
        -- and a commit that nothing wanted. One predicate answers both, so
        -- the two can not drift into disagreeing about whether a given task
        -- may wrap up.
}

rule ExitSessionViaMcp {
    when: McpExitSession(task, token?, action?, pr_url?)

    -- Checked by the tool before any call: there is nothing to ask the module
    -- without them.
    requires: token != null
    requires: action != null

    -- Since store-split M5 the tool ASKS: one request_exit_session call
    -- (store-split.allium: RequestExitSession). The module checks the token
    -- against the request rows, and in the same transaction applies the
    -- close (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSession), consumes the token, runs the
    -- epic chain (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask) and writes the
    -- exit_session row whose host tears the window down
    -- (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown).
    ensures: ExitSessionRequested(task: task, token: token, action: action, pr_url: pr_url)

    @guidance
        -- Single-call session close for all three wrap-up actions. The old
        -- two-phase behaviour (first call showed a reflection prompt and set
        -- token.reflected, second call closed) is gone: the mandatory
        -- reflection is now the /retro skill, which the /wrap-up skill runs
        -- BEFORE exit_session is ever called.
        --
        -- Validation, in order (all mutate nothing on failure; every refusal
        -- is an INVALID_PARAMS error):
        --   • in the tool: a missing token → "no exit token — call wrap_up
        --     first"; a missing action → "action is required — pass the same
        --     action used in wrap_up".
        --   • in the module (RequestExitSession), carrying its words: no
        --     unconsumed done wrap_up row for the task (whatever the token
        --     names), or the token's row already consumed →
        --     "no exit token — call wrap_up first"; a token naming another
        --     row, superseded by a newer wrap_up, or voided by a dispatch of
        --     the task that finished after the token's row did
        --     (store-split.allium, W3) → "invalid exit token"; an
        --     action that differs from the row's → an error naming both
        --     actions; current_agent(task).tmux_window already null → "task #<id> has no
        --     active session", which the /wrap-up skill treats as success
        --     (some other path already tore the session down; on a normal
        --     double-close the token has also been consumed, so a retry hits
        --     the "call wrap_up first" branch first); action pr without a
        --     pr_url → "pr_url is required for action 'pr' — pass the URL
        --     returned by `gh pr create`"; pr_url is ignored for rebase and
        --     done.
        --
        -- Terminal mutation, applied by the module atomically with clearing
        -- tmux_window and consuming the token (pr-workflow.allium:
        -- ExitSession):
        --   • rebase / done → task.status = done, task.completed_at = now from
        --                      the store clock (the same completion stamp as
        --                      tasks/ConfirmDone)
        --   • pr            → task.status = review, task.url = TaskUrl(pr_url,
        --                      url_type = pr) — this branch does not enter done,
        --                      so task.completed_at is untouched
        -- The module rolls up the parent epic chain in the same transaction;
        -- boards redraw from their subscriptions. Handled by
        -- handle_exit_session in src/mcp/handlers/tasks/wrap_up.rs.
        --
        -- == The response ==
        -- Accepted: the close took effect. The response is "Session closed."
        -- or, when the epic chain requested a subtask (the exit_session row's
        -- chained_task), "Session closed. Dispatching next epic subtask #<id>
        -- '<title>'."
        --
        -- DECISION (with the user, store-split M5): the tool does NOT wait for
        -- the window teardown. It answers as soon as the module accepts the
        -- close. The teardown kills this agent's own window, so a reply that
        -- waited for it would never be read, and a failed teardown does not
        -- undo the close anyway (store-split.allium, W4, W5). The teardown's
        -- outcome is recorded on the exit_session row, not in the response.
        --
        -- Refused: the error above. Nothing changed, the token is still
        -- valid, and the agent fixes the cause and calls again — CHANGED in
        -- M5: before, the token was consumed ahead of a terminal write that
        -- could fail, so a failed close was reported as a success telling the
        -- agent to stop and hand back to the human. That state no longer
        -- exists (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSession, "A close that does not
        -- take effect").
        --
        -- No answer from the store (a transport error): the call may or may
        -- not have landed. The tool settles it from the rows: an exit_session
        -- row that consumed this token means the close took effect, and the
        -- response is the accepted one; no such row means it did not, and
        -- the response is an error saying the close did not take effect, the
        -- tmux session is still alive and the token is still valid, so call
        -- exit_session again. Neither response claims a teardown that has
        -- not happened. These reactions live in the response, which reaches
        -- every caller with or without the /wrap-up skill loaded, and in the
        -- skill's exit_session-errors reference file; the tool description
        -- does not carry them (TheNextCallIsNamedJustInTime).
        --
        -- Why the pr path closes the PR-merge race: a PR task never enters
        -- review with a pr-typed url until the exact same transaction that
        -- also nulls tmux_window, so PollPrStatus (which filters on status =
        -- review and url.url_type = pr) can never observe it mid-session.
        --
        -- The close emits SessionClosed, which drives automatic epic chaining
        -- (AutoDispatchNextSubtask in epics.allium) in the same transaction.
        -- A chain that stops, or fails, never turns a successful close into
        -- an error. All three actions chain — for action = pr the finished
        -- work sits on a PR branch rather than base_branch, so the chained
        -- subtask still will not see it, but that is inherent to choosing the
        -- PR path rather than something this close does wrong. There is no
        -- agent-facing tool to dispatch the next subtask. The tool
        -- description states the positive fact — that exit_session starts the
        -- epic's next backlog subtask when the epic auto-dispatches — which is
        -- what an agent needs to not go looking for one.
}

rule SetVerifyCommandViaMcp {
    when: McpSetVerifyCommand(repo_path, command?)

    -- A command may not span multiple lines; embedded newlines or
    -- carriage returns are rejected at the handler boundary. This
    -- upholds core's VerifyCommandSingleLine invariant before the value
    -- is ever stored.
    requires:
        if command != null:
            not (command.contains("\n") or command.contains("\r"))

    ensures: core/SavedRepoPath.created_or_updated(
        path: repo_path,
        verify_command: if command = null or is_blank(command): null else: command
    )

    @guidance
        -- set_verify_command stores (or clears) the verify command
        -- associated with a repo_path in saved_repo_paths.verify_command.
        -- A blank or whitespace-only command is normalised to null (clear)
        -- by the DB layer, matching the trait contract.
        -- A command containing "\n" or "\r" is rejected at the handler
        -- with JSON-RPC -32602 (invalid params) before reaching the DB,
        -- which would otherwise surface the constraint as -32603.
        -- Nothing nudges an agent to call this tool: exit_session carries no
        -- prompt about it, and the agent only runs an already-recorded command
        -- (AgentsRunTheVerifyCommand). Recording a verify command is an explicit act by
        -- an agent or operator (see the `dispatch repo set-verify` CLI above).
        --
        -- == The tool description does not claim prompt injection ==
        -- The description states that the command never appears in a dispatch
        -- prompt, names the surface that does carry it (get_task's "Verify
        -- command" line) and says the agent runs it itself. It said the opposite for a time — that the command
        -- was injected into future agent prompts — which contradicts the
        -- prompt-assembly rule in dispatch-prompt.allium and sends the one reader who
        -- needs the command looking on the single surface that never carries
        -- it. A description that disagrees with the prompt rule is a defect in
        -- the description, not a second opinion.
        -- Handled by handle_set_verify_command in
        -- src/mcp/handlers/tasks/verify.rs.
}

-- == MCP context resources ==
--
-- The dispatch MCP server (the one the TUI process runs) offers read-only
-- context to its callers as MCP resources: the agent-facing skills and their
-- reference files, the validated learnings, the caller's own task, and any
-- task by id. Five URI shapes, nothing else:
--
--   kind        URI                                 resolves when
--   ----------  ----------------------------------  --------------------------
--   skill       skill://<name>/SKILL.md             <name> is the frontmatter
--                                                   name of a skill in
--                                                   dispatch's built-in copy
--                                                   of plugin/skills
--   skill_ref   skill://<name>/references/<file>.md that skill's built-in copy
--                                                   has references/<file>.md
--   learning    dispatch://learnings/<id>           a core/Learning with that
--                                                   id exists and is validated
--                                                   (status = approved)
--   own_task    dispatch://task/self                the caller is a dispatched
--                                                   agent (caller_identity
--                                                   Task(t)) and task t exists
--   task        dispatch://task/<id>                a core/Task with that id
--                                                   exists; <id> is canonical
--                                                   decimal, no leading zeros
--
-- A task URI is read by id and never listed: the listing offers only the
-- caller's own task, so a caller finds another task's id elsewhere (an epic,
-- a sibling, get_task) and reads it here. It serves the same text get_task
-- returns for that task, whoever the caller is. dispatch://task/self is the
-- literal word "self", never an id, so the two shapes cannot overlap.
--
-- Decided in #28716 and deliberately not built:
--   • the io.modelcontextprotocol/skills extension (skills/list, skills/get):
--     the MCP client matrix does not list Claude Code as supporting it
--     (checked 2026-10-07; only mcpc is full, Inspector and others partial).
--     Revisit when Claude Code is listed. Skills stay ordinary skill://
--     resources until then.
--   • repo docs and Allium specs as resources: agents read them from their
--     own worktree, which is always the right branch; a served copy could
--     come from another.
--
-- context_uri_kind(uri) classifies a URI by its shape alone, or as
-- unrecognised for every other URI — any other scheme, any other dispatch://
-- path, a malformed id. An unrecognised URI never resolves. A skill_ref
-- <file> is a single file name inside the skill's references/ directory: a
-- path containing `..`, an absolute path, or anything else that would name a
-- file outside that skill's directory is unrecognised, so it resolves as
-- unknown rather than being followed.
--
-- context_uri_resolves(uri, caller_identity) is exactly that table: it is
-- true for a URI of one of the five kinds that meets its condition, and false
-- for every other URI. It is evaluated at read time, so a learning deleted or
-- archived after it was listed, or a caller whose task has since been deleted,
-- reads as unknown.
--
-- Agents are first-class MCP users: Claude Code does not load MCP resources
-- into an agent's context by itself today, so every resource is also reachable
-- through a tool, read_context, which returns the same text. A read carries
-- `via` — resources_read or read_context — naming the entry point it arrived
-- through; it chooses the response envelope — for a success and for a
-- refusal — and nothing else. The two entry
-- points share one identity check, one resolution (ResolveContextUri) and one
-- refusal (RejectUnresolvedContextUri), so they cannot disagree about who may
-- read, which URIs exist, or what they say.
--
-- Caller identity. resources/list, resources/read and read_context all
-- consume caller identity (dispatch://task/self and the listing depend on it),
-- so all three reject a missing or malformed identity with JSON-RPC -32600,
-- echoing the request's id — the same answer every tools/call gives (see
-- CreateTaskViaMcp's guidance). caller_identity_valid(caller_identity) below
-- is that check. initialize and tools/list stay identity-free.
--
-- Nothing here writes: no resource read mutates domain state, and the server
-- sends no resource notifications (no list_changed, no subscribe/updated).

rule DeclareResourcesCapability {
    when: McpInitialize(caller_identity)

    ensures: McpCapabilitiesDeclared(tools: true, resources: true)

    @guidance
        -- initialize's response declares two capabilities: tools (as today)
        -- and resources. The resources capability declares neither
        -- `subscribe` nor `listChanged` — the server sends no resource
        -- notifications (ContextResourcesAreReadOnly). It does NOT declare
        -- the io.modelcontextprotocol/skills extension; serving skills
        -- through that extension is a later task (#28716). Until then a
        -- skill is an ordinary resource with a skill:// URI.
        -- initialize stays identity-free, as CreateTaskViaMcp's guidance
        -- describes: the declared capabilities do not depend on the caller.
}

rule ServerInstructionsStateDispatchConventions {
    when: McpInitialize(caller_identity)

    ensures: McpServerInstructionsSent(text: server_instructions())

    @guarantee InstructionsCoverOnlyConventionsNoSchemaStates
        -- initialize's response carries an `instructions` string. Claude Code
        -- loads it into the system prompt of every session connected to the
        -- server, so it is the one place to state a convention that spans
        -- several tools and that no single tool's schema owns:
        --   * the name of one of a task's agents works wherever a tool takes
        --     a task id (TaskRefResolvesToTaskId; agents.allium:
        --     AgentNameResolvesToTask);
        --   * an agent's Claude Code session carries that name, so another
        --     agent is reached with the harness's ListAgents and SendMessage
        --     by it, and each task's details and listing show it
        --     (agents.allium: PromptNamesTheAgent);
        --   * tasks in the same epic are siblings.
        --
        -- It says nothing else. It is identity-free like the rest of the
        -- initialize response (DeclareResourcesCapability), so every client
        -- reads it: an operator session, an agent in another repo, a task with
        -- no epic. A rule that holds only for some tasks, or that is a
        -- repository's policy rather than how to use dispatch's tools — TDD,
        -- Allium specs, the design step, when to wrap up — stays in the
        -- dispatch prompt (dispatch-prompt.allium), whose conditions decide
        -- whether it applies. What one tool does belongs in that tool's
        -- description (the tool-description standard at the head of this
        -- file), not here.
        --
        -- The dispatch prompt does not restate these conventions: the epic
        -- section keeps the epic's id and title and the invitation to ask a
        -- sibling for help, and leaves how to reach one to this text
        -- (dispatch-prompt.allium: ThePromptNamesNoToolMerelyToSayItExists).
}

rule ListContextResourcesViaMcp {
    when: McpListResources(caller_identity, cursor?)

    requires: caller_identity_valid(caller_identity)
    requires: cursor = null or context_list_cursor_valid(cursor)

    let has_own_task =
        caller_identity.kind = task and core/Task.exists(caller_identity.task_id)

    ensures: ContextResourcesListed(
        caller_identity: caller_identity,
        skills: built_in_skills(),
        learnings: core/Learnings where status = approved,
        lists_own_task: has_own_task,
        cursor: cursor,
        page_size: config.context_list_page_size
    )

    @guidance
        -- The full listing, in this fixed order:
        --   1. dispatch://task/self, only when the caller is a dispatched
        --      agent whose task exists. A Session caller does not see it.
        --      mimeType text/plain; name "Your task"; description the task's
        --      title.
        --   2. the skills, by skill name ascending. Each skill contributes its
        --      skill://<name>/SKILL.md entry, then one
        --      skill://<name>/references/<file>.md entry per file in its
        --      references/ directory, by file name ascending. All are
        --      mimeType text/markdown. SKILL.md's entry is named <name> and
        --      described by the frontmatter description; a reference entry is
        --      named "<name>: <file>" with no description.
        --   3. one dispatch://learnings/<id> entry per validated learning, by
        --      id ascending — ALL of them, with no filtering by the caller's
        --      repo, epic or task. The scope a learning carries governs
        --      dispatch-prompt injection (learnings.allium: Scope Model), not
        --      this listing. Archived and rejected learnings are not listed.
        --      mimeType text/plain; name a short title cut from the
        --      learning's summary; description the summary's first line.
        -- No other task appears in the listing. Everything listed is readable;
        -- the one readable set left unlisted is task URIs by id (see "task"
        -- above).
        --
        -- Pagination. resources/list returns at most
        -- config.context_list_page_size entries per call. When more remain,
        -- the result carries nextCursor; the caller passes it back as
        -- params.cursor to get the next page, and the last page carries no
        -- nextCursor. The cursor is opaque to the caller. It records a
        -- position in the order above, not an offset, so an entry added or
        -- removed between pages (a learning recorded, archived or deleted)
        -- causes no other entry to be skipped or repeated: each page resumes
        -- after the last entry the previous page returned. A cursor the
        -- server cannot decode into a position in the listing order is refused
        -- with -32602 (cursors are not signed, so one the server never issued
        -- but that names a valid position is accepted).
        --
        -- A missing or malformed caller identity is refused with -32600 (see
        -- "Caller identity" above). Listing is read-only: it records no
        -- core/Retrieval and touches no learning's counters.
}

rule ReadContextResourceViaMcp {
    when: McpReadResource(caller_identity, uri)

    requires: caller_identity_valid(caller_identity)

    ensures: ContextReadRequested(
        caller_identity: caller_identity,
        uri: uri,
        via: resources_read
    )

    @guidance
        -- resources/read(uri). The response carries one content item for the
        -- URI, with the text and mimeType ResolveContextUri produces. A
        -- missing or malformed caller identity is refused with -32600 before
        -- the URI is looked at.
}

rule ReadContextViaMcp {
    when: McpReadContext(caller_identity, uri)

    requires: caller_identity_valid(caller_identity)

    ensures: ContextReadRequested(
        caller_identity: caller_identity,
        uri: uri,
        via: read_context
    )

    @guidance
        -- read_context(uri) — uri is REQUIRED. The tool twin of
        -- resources/read: its success result is a text content block
        -- carrying exactly the text resources/read returns for the same URI
        -- and caller. It is refused under exactly the conditions
        -- resources/read is, with the same message text, but in the
        -- existing tool-error envelope: a refused URI (unrecognised, unknown,
        -- no own task) or an internal failure comes back as a tool result
        -- with isError: true carrying that message, not as a JSON-RPC error
        -- — the tools/call dispatcher wraps every tool error that way, and
        -- read_context is no exception. The one JSON-RPC error it gives is
        -- -32600 for a missing or malformed identity, which tools/call
        -- rejects before any tool runs, so before that wrap. It exists because
        -- Claude Code cannot pull an MCP resource into an agent's context by
        -- itself today, and agents are first-class MCP users: a capability
        -- reachable only as a resource is one an agent cannot use.
        -- Description standard (Tool descriptions, above): the description
        -- says that read_context returns the text of a dispatch context
        -- resource and names the URI shapes; it does not restate the
        -- resolution table or the error codes.
        -- Being a tools/call, a read_context call is counted in feature usage
        -- like every other tool (QueryUsageViaMcp). That counter is not
        -- domain state; ContextResourcesAreReadOnly is about the resources.
        -- For a task caller, tools/call also appends the call and its result
        -- text to the agent's trajectory, like every tool; resources/read
        -- records nothing. The trajectory is not domain state either.
        -- read_context rejects unknown arguments like every other tool;
        -- TheToolTwinServesTheSameText is about the URI, not extra arguments.
}

rule ResolveContextUri {
    when: ContextReadRequested(caller_identity, uri, via)

    requires: context_uri_resolves(uri, caller_identity)

    let kind = context_uri_kind(uri)

    ensures:
        if kind = skill:
            ContextServed(via: via, uri: uri, mime_type: "text/markdown",
                          text: built_in_skill_text(skill_name_of(uri)))
        else if kind = skill_ref:
            ContextServed(via: via, uri: uri, mime_type: "text/markdown",
                          text: built_in_skill_reference_text(
                              skill_name_of(uri), reference_file_of(uri)))
        else if kind = learning:
            ContextServed(via: via, uri: uri, mime_type: "text/plain",
                          text: learning_resource_text(learning_id_of(uri)))
        else if kind = own_task:
            ContextServed(via: via, uri: uri, mime_type: "text/plain",
                          text: task_detail_text(caller_identity.task_id))
        else if kind = task:
            ContextServed(via: via, uri: uri, mime_type: "text/plain",
                          text: task_detail_text(task_id_of(uri)))

    @guidance
        -- What each kind serves:
        --   • skill / skill_ref — the file from the built-in copy, byte for
        --     byte (SKILL.md with its frontmatter).
        --   • learning — the learning's text: its summary, then its detail
        --     when it has one. Nothing else of the row is part of the text.
        --   • own_task — the same task detail GetTaskViaMcp returns for the
        --     caller's own task (task_detail_text is that rendering, so the
        --     two cannot drift): every line get_task renders, in its order,
        --     including [Verify command] and any "Wrap-up: refused" line.
        --     The URI takes no id: "self" is always the caller, resolved from
        --     transport identity, never from anything in the request.
        --   • task — the same task_detail_text rendering, for the task the id
        --     names. Any caller may read any task. The rendering is
        --     get_task's, so it carries no trajectory, usage data or host
        --     identity.
        -- Read-only: no rule fires on a served read, and a learning read this
        -- way records no core/Retrieval (ContextReadsAreNotRetrievals).
}

rule RejectUnresolvedContextUri {
    when: ContextReadRequested(caller_identity, uri, via)

    requires: not context_uri_resolves(uri, caller_identity)

    ensures: ContextReadRejected(via: via, uri: uri,
                                 message: unresolved_context_uri_message(uri, caller_identity))

    @guidance
        -- Every URI that does not resolve is refused on both entry points
        -- with one message, which names the URI. Only the envelope depends on
        -- via:
        --   resources_read — a JSON-RPC error, -32602 (invalid params).
        --   read_context   — a tool result with isError: true whose text is
        --                    the message (the existing tool-error
        --                    convention; see ReadContextViaMcp).
        -- That covers an unrecognised URI (any other scheme or path,
        -- including a skill reference path that escapes the skill's
        -- directory), a skill or reference file the built-in copy does not
        -- have, a learning id that never existed, was deleted, or is archived
        -- or rejected, a task id that names no task, and dispatch://task/self from a caller without a task.
        -- The refusal does not say WHICH of those it was beyond naming the
        -- URI, with one exception: dispatch://task/self from a caller
        -- without a task says so plainly — the caller is not a dispatched
        -- agent, so it has no own task to read — because that caller can do
        -- nothing with a bare "unknown resource".
        -- A learning that is unvalidated and one that never existed get the
        -- same answer, so the refusal reveals nothing about learnings that
        -- are not served.
        -- resources/read uses -32602 deliberately, not the -32001 not-found
        -- code the task tools use for an unknown task_id: here the URI IS the
        -- parameter, and one code covers every way it can fail to name a
        -- resource.
        -- An internal failure while producing a resolvable resource's text
        -- (a store read that fails, for instance) is -32603 on resources/read,
        -- never -32602, because the caller asked for something that exists;
        -- on read_context it is an isError result carrying the same message.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Any client of the dispatch MCP server: a dispatched agent (caller_identity
-- Task(t)) or a session (caller_identity Session). Code, not a person.
actor McpClient {
    identified_by: McpCaller where connected = true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The context resources boundary. See "MCP context resources" in Rules.
surface McpContextResources {
    facing client: McpClient

    provides:
        McpInitialize(client)
        McpListResources(client, cursor?)
        McpReadResource(client, uri)
        McpReadContext(client, uri)

    @guarantee ResourcesCapabilityIsDeclared
        -- initialize declares the resources capability beside tools, and
        -- resources/list and resources/read are answered. The
        -- io.modelcontextprotocol/skills extension is not declared.

    @guarantee ListedMeansReadable
        -- At any one moment, every URI resources/list returns to a caller,
        -- across all its pages, resolves for that caller on resources/read
        -- and read_context. Task URIs by id resolve without being listed. A listed URI can stop
        -- resolving later (a learning archived or deleted), and then reads as
        -- unknown.

    @guarantee ListingPagesHaveNoGapsOrRepeats
        -- The listing has one stable order — the own task, then skills by
        -- name (each SKILL.md before its references, by file name), then
        -- learnings by id ascending — and a cursor resumes after the last
        -- entry it was issued for. Paging through to the end returns every
        -- entry that existed throughout exactly once, whatever changes
        -- between pages. An invalid cursor is -32602.

    @guarantee TheToolTwinServesTheSameText
        -- For every URI and caller identity — present, missing or malformed —
        -- read_context succeeds exactly when resources/read does and returns
        -- the same text, and is refused under exactly the same conditions
        -- with the same message text. Only the error envelope differs:
        --   condition                      resources/read   read_context
        --   -----------------------------  ---------------  -----------------
        --   missing or malformed identity  -32600           -32600
        --   URI does not resolve           -32602           isError result
        --   internal failure               -32603           isError result
        -- read_context follows the existing tool-error convention (every
        -- tool error is a tool result with isError: true); identity is the
        -- exception because tools/call rejects it before any tool runs.

    @guarantee IdentityIsRequired
        -- resources/list, resources/read and read_context each refuse a
        -- missing or malformed caller identity with -32600, as tools/call
        -- does. initialize and tools/list do not consult identity.

    @guarantee SkillsComeFromTheBuiltInCopy
        -- A skill resource's text — SKILL.md or a references/ file — is the
        -- file in dispatch's built-in copy of plugin/skills, the repo's source
        -- of truth. The installed copy under ~/.claude/plugins/ is never
        -- read, so what an agent reads cannot depend on what happens to be
        -- installed on the host. No skill URI reaches a file outside its
        -- skill's directory.

    @guarantee SelfIsAlwaysTheCaller
        -- dispatch://task/self names the caller's own task, as the transport
        -- identifies the caller, and nothing a request carries can point it
        -- at another task. Another task is reachable only by its own
        -- dispatch://task/<id> URI.

    @guarantee ContextResourcesAreReadOnly
        -- No list or read changes domain state: no task, epic or learning is
        -- written, no core/Retrieval is recorded, and the server sends no
        -- resource notifications. There is no resource write method.

    @guarantee ContextReadsAreNotRetrievals
        -- Reading a learning as a context resource records no
        -- core/Retrieval, so it does not make that learning rateable:
        -- rate_learning still requires a retrieval (learnings.allium:
        -- RateLearningViaMcp), which only the dispatch prompt's injection
        -- and query_learnings record. An agent that wants to rate a learning
        -- it found here reaches it through query_learnings first.

    @guarantee NeverServedDataIsUnreachable
        -- These are never served, under any URI, by resources/list,
        -- resources/read or read_context, whatever the caller:
        --   • agent trajectories (the recorded tool-call history of a task)
        --   • feature-usage data (what query_usage aggregates)
        --   • the host's identity, or the host file it lives in
        --   • database or store paths and addresses
        --   • any epic, and any task's trajectory (a task is served only as
        --     get_task renders it)
        --   • repository files — docs, specs, plans, source — and any file
        --     outside a built-in skill's own directory
        --   • learnings that are not validated (archived or rejected)
        -- This holds by construction: context_uri_resolves recognises five
        -- URI shapes and nothing else, none of them maps to any of the data
        -- above, a skill reference path that escapes its skill's directory
        -- is unrecognised, and the task shapes serve get_task's
        -- rendering of a task, which carries none of it. Adding a
        -- resource kind is a change to this spec, not to a handler alone.

    @guarantee UnknownUrisAreInvalidParams
        -- A URI that does not resolve — unrecognised, escaping a skill's
        -- directory, deleted, archived, rejected, or task/self
        -- with no caller task — is refused with a message naming the URI.
        -- resources/read answers with JSON-RPC -32602; read_context answers
        -- with an isError tool result carrying the same message. An internal
        -- failure serving a resolvable URI is -32603 on resources/read and an
        -- isError result on read_context.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "update_tasks' typed-url contract is now to be stated by the schema: url_type as an enum, and url_type required whenever url is non-empty. The second is a conditional requirement (JSON Schema if/then or a top-level combinator), and tool input schemas reaching Claude may not accept one at the top level. If the conditional cannot be expressed, does the url description keep one clause stating the dependency, or does the contract live only in the validation error?"

open question "update_tasks naming a specific repo-group sub-epic in epic_id while also changing repo_path to a different repo: UpdateTaskViaMcp's ensures says the assignment is direct (route_target of a sub-epic is the sub-epic), but since store-split M2 the relink is written first and patch_task's repo change then re-routes the task to the sub-epic for the new repo, so the explicit sub-epic is overridden (and deleted if left empty). Before M2 the explicit sub-epic won. Which is intended: does an explicit sub-epic win over the new repo, or does the repo always decide inside a grouped subtree?"
